{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba 'docs/design/dao.stream.datagram.md'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Datagram: The Raw Datagram Layer\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented (DHT epic slice S1). Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement, and to the Host Boundaries section of\n     6\t[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). Every sentence below is a rule.\n     7\t\n     8\t## 1. Place in the stack\n     9\t\n    10\tThe governing direction, in the owner's words:\n    11\t\n    12\t> udp datagram api should be foundational and dao.stream on top and the dht\n    13\t> is built on a udp base dao.stream\n    14\t\n    15\t```\n    16\thost socket (DatagramSocket | dgram | RawDatagramSocket)\n    17\t  └─ dao.stream.datagram        one bound socket as dao.stream values: this document\n    18\t       ├─ dao.stream.udp        decoded values, fragmentation, projection (dao.stream.remote.md 3.2)\n    19\t       │    └─ dao.stream.remote   mirror, reflection, link\n    20\t       └─ dao.jing.dht          Kademlia wire, chunks, hardening (dao.jing.dht.md)\n    21\t```\n    22\t\n    23\tA datagram socket is exposed as two ordinary stream ends: a **writer handle**\n    24\twhose `append!` hands one addressed datagram to the socket, and a\n    25\t**traffic stream**, supplied by the composition, onto which the host adapter\n    26\tdeposits every received datagram as one event. The socket retains nothing, so\n    27\tit has no reader surface (`dao.stream.md`, Surfaces); readers hold their own\n    28\tcursors on the traffic stream.\n    29\t\n    30\tThis layer carries bytes and addresses and nothing else. It does not decode,\n    31\tretry, order, deduplicate, fragment, reassemble, resolve names, or traverse\n    32\tNATs. Each of those is an interpreter above it.\n    33\t\n    34\t## 2. Values\n    35\t\n    36\tAll keys are under `:dao.stream.datagram/`. Every value is plain data and\n    37\tsurvives any `dao.stream` codec, Transit-JSON included.\n    38\t\n    39\t```clojure\n    40\t;; outbound: the value append! takes\n    41\t{:dao.stream.datagram/destination {:dao.stream.datagram/host \"203.0.113.7\"\n    42\t                                   :dao.stream.datagram/port 4100}\n    43\t :dao.stream.datagram/bytes       \"<base64>\"}\n    44\t\n    45\t;; inbound: one event per received datagram\n    46\t{:dao.stream.datagram/socket id                ; the socket's logical identity\n    47\t :dao.stream.datagram/source {:dao.stream.datagram/host \"198.51.100.2\"\n    48\t                              :dao.stream.datagram/port 53122}\n    49\t :dao.stream.datagram/bytes  \"<base64>\"}\n    50\t\n    51\t;; lifecycle: one event per host fact about the socket itself\n    52\t{:dao.stream.datagram/socket id\n    53\t :dao.stream.datagram/event  :dao.stream.datagram/bound        ; | /bind-failed\n    54\t                                                               ; | /send-failed\n    55\t                                                               ; | /closed\n    56\t :dao.stream.datagram/local  {:dao.stream.datagram/host h      ; bound only\n    57\t                              :dao.stream.datagram/port p}\n    58\t :dao.stream.datagram/reason \"<text>\"}                         ; failures only\n    59\t```\n    60\t\n    61\t- `:dao.stream.datagram/bytes` is padded standard-alphabet Base64 of the\n    62\t  exact datagram payload: no whitespace, no URL-safe alphabet. A zero-length\n    63\t  datagram is the empty string. Host byte arrays never cross onto a stream\n    64\t  (`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries); Base64 is what lets a traffic stream be\n    65\t  served over any channel.\n    66\t- `:dao.stream.datagram/host` is always an **IP literal** in its textual form,\n    67\t  IPv4 or IPv6. A source host is the address the socket observed. A\n    68\t  destination host that is not an IP literal is `invalid-value`: name\n    69\t  resolution can wait, and no operation waits. Resolution is a separate\n    70\t  interpreter or host policy that produces literals.\n    71\t- `:dao.stream.datagram/port` is an integer in 1..65535.\n    72\t- A datagram event carries `:dao.stream.datagram/bytes` and\n    73\t  `:dao.stream.datagram/source`; a lifecycle event carries\n    74\t  `:dao.stream.datagram/event`. A value carrying neither is not this layer's.\n    75\t\n    76\tThe strict Base64 codec lives at or below `dao.stream` (one portable helper\n    77\tnamespace, `dao.stream.base64`), and `dao.jing` uses that same helper. This\n    78\tlayer never requires `dao.jing`: a boundary depends on nothing above it.\n    79\t\n    80\t## 3. The socket, its identity and its descriptor\n    81\t\n    82\tBinding is a host act, not `create!`. On Node and Dart the bound port of an\n    83\tephemeral bind is not knowable until the host answers, so identity and\n    84\treachability cannot both be settled at once, which `dao.stream.md` (Creation\n    85\tand Attachment) requires of `create!`. The composition therefore proceeds in\n    86\ttwo explicit moves.\n    87\t\n    88\t1. **Bind.** The host seam (`dao.stream.datagram.jvm`, `.node`, `.dart`)\n    89\t   takes `{:identity id :deposit <writer> :bind-host h :bind-port p\n    90\t   :max-bytes n}` and returns at once `{:send! f :close! f}`. `id` is a\n    91\t   string the composition mints. `:bind-host` is the local interface address\n    92\t   (the wildcard address is legal); `:bind-port` 0 asks for an ephemeral\n    93\t   port. The seam takes the deposit writer and nothing else from above: it is\n    94\t   handed no function to invoke.\n    95\t2. **Observe `bound`, then compose the writer.** The seam deposits\n    96\t   `:dao.stream.datagram/bound` with the actual local address, on every host,\n    97\t   synchronous bind or not. The composition reads that event and calls the\n    98\t   portable constructor `dao.stream.datagram/writer` with the seam and the\n    99\t   complete descriptor. A failed bind deposits `:bind-failed` and there is no\n   100\t   writer.\n   101\t\n   102\tThe descriptor:\n   103\t\n   104\t```clojure\n   105\t{:dao.stream/type                :dao.stream/datagram\n   106\t :dao.stream/identity            id\n   107\t :dao.stream.datagram/bind-host  h     ; the LOCAL interface this socket is bound on\n   108\t :dao.stream.datagram/bind-port  p}    ; the LOCAL port actually bound, never 0\n   109\t```\n   110\t\n   111\t- The keys say `bind-` because they name the **local** socket. The UDP value\n   112\t  channel's descriptor keys, `:dao.stream.udp/host` and `:dao.stream.udp/port`\n   113\t  (`dao.stream.remote.md` 3.2), name the **remote destination** its writer\n   114\t  sends toward. The two descriptors appear side by side in one composition and\n   115\t  must never be read as the same kind of address.\n   116\t- A bind address is not a reachable address. Behind a NAT the address peers\n   117\t  observe differs from both keys; nothing here claims otherwise, and no\n   118\t  consumer advertises `bind-host`/`bind-port` as where it can be reached.\n   119\t- `attach!` on this descriptor off its host is `:dao.stream/not-found`, as a\n   120\t  ring buffer's is. On its own host it resolves only against sockets a\n   121\t  composition chose to keep in its host-composed attach closure; there is no\n   122\t  registry. There is no `:dao.stream/create` entry.\n   123\t\n   124\t## 4. The writer handle\n   125\t\n   126\tDeclared surface: `#{:writer :closable}`. `descriptor` answers `ok` with the\n   127\tdescriptor above and its identity, before and after close.\n   128\t\n   129\t`append!` outcomes:\n   130\t\n   131\t| Outcome | When |\n   132\t|---|---|\n   133\t| `:dao.stream/ok` | The datagram was handed to the socket. It asserts nothing about the wire, the path, or the destination. |\n   134\t| `:dao.stream/invalid-value` | Not an outbound value of section 2: a malformed destination, a host that is not an IP literal, bytes that are not strict Base64, or a decoded length over `:max-bytes`. Nothing was sent. |\n   135\t| `:dao.stream/closed` | The socket was closed. Nothing was sent. |\n   136\t| `:dao.stream/transport-error` | The host send failed synchronously. Failures are clean: nothing was sent. |\n   137\t\n   138\tExcluded outcomes, with reasons: `full`, because this layer holds no outbound\n   139\tqueue and a socket under pressure loses datagrams, which is the medium's own\n   140\tdeclared nature, not a refusal; `refused`, because no policy is composed in\n   141\tthis layer (a gate composed over the handle may produce it,\n   142\t`dao.stream.middleware.md`).\n   143\t\n   144\tA host whose send reports failure only later (Node, Dart) deposits\n   145\t`:dao.stream.datagram/send-failed` on the traffic stream; that is the channel\n   146\tthis transport declares for an answer it cannot give at call time\n   147\t(`dao.stream.md`, Writing).\n   148\t\n   149\t`close!` answers `ok`, closes the host socket, and is idempotent. The seam\n   150\tdeposits `:dao.stream.datagram/closed` when it still can; the irreducible\n   151\tcorner of `dao.stream.md` (Surfaces, observably gone) applies when it cannot.\n   152\t\n   153\t`:max-bytes` is composition data, default 1200, and is symmetric: an inbound\n   154\tdatagram longer than it is dropped below the transform as protocol\n   155\tvalidation, counted and never deposited. 1200 clears the IPv6 minimum path\n   156\tMTU with headers to spare.\n   157\t\n   158\t## 5. The traffic stream\n   159\t\n   160\t- The deposit destination is supplied by the composition and **must be an\n   161\t  evicting ring** (`dao.stream.ringbuffer`). A complete-history log under a\n   162\t  network-facing socket is a host assembly defect: any sender on the network\n   163\t  could grow it without bound.\n   164\t- A ring never refuses, so the deposit-admission rule of `dao.stream.md`\n   165\t  (Surfaces) holds by construction.\n   166\t- A `:dao.stream/gap` on the traffic stream means datagrams were lost to this\n   167\t  reader. It is indistinguishable from network loss and is handled the same\n   168\t  way, by whatever loss policy the reading interpreter already has.\n   169\t- Capacity is counted in events. One event is at most `:max-bytes` of payload\n   170\t  as Base64, so memory is bounded by `capacity * ceil(max-bytes * 4 / 3)` plus\n   171\t  envelope.\n   172\t- The seam's transform deposits and returns. It judges no datagram and\n   173\t  invokes nothing above itself.\n   174\t\n   175\t## 6. Peer observers on one socket\n   176\t\n   177\tAny number of interpreters may read one traffic stream, each with its own\n   178\tcursor, and may write through the one writer handle. None knows the others\n   179\texist. A datagram that is not an interpreter's own is dropped by that\n   180\tinterpreter without reply and without diagnostics on any shared stream.\n   181\t\n   182\tTwo interpreters are specified today and may share a socket, which is what\n   183\tlets a hole-punched mapping (`dao.stream.remote.md` section 4) serve both:\n   184\t\n   185\t- `dao.jing.dht` owns exactly the datagrams whose decoded value is a map\n   186\t  carrying `:dao.jing.dht/v` (`dao.jing.dht.md`, The wire).\n   187\t- `dao.stream.udp` owns every other decodable value, and drops a map carrying\n   188\t  `:dao.jing.dht/v` before its deposit.\n   189\t\n   190\tA further interpreter on a shared socket declares its own discriminator here.\n   191\t\n   192\t## 7. `dao.stream.udp` rebuilt on this layer\n   193\t\n   194\t`dao.stream.udp` keeps its public surface: the descriptor, `make-port`,\n   195\t`send-value!`, `send-to!`, `receive!`, `projection`, `step!`,\n   196\t`make-attacher`, the fragment envelope, and both reassembly bounds. What\n   197\tchanges is what feeds it.\n   198\t\n   199\t- `make-port`'s `:send!` is composed as a closure that encodes the bytes as\n   200\t  Base64 and appends one outbound value to the raw writer. `send-value!`\n   201\t  answers the raw writer's first non-`ok` outcome instead of discarding it.\n   202\t- **`port-step!`** is new: one step that reads the raw traffic stream from\n   203\t  the port's own raw cursor to `blocked`, bounded by a budget, skips lifecycle\n   204\t  events and `:dao.jing.dht/v` maps, decodes each datagram event's Base64, and\n   205\t  calls `receive!` with the source address and bytes. A raw `gap` adopts the\n   206\t  recovery cursor: those datagrams are lost, and the link's resend rule\n   207\t  recovers exactly as it does for network loss.\n   208\t- One owner per port drives `port-step!`, and the composition drives it\n   209\t  before the projections' `step!`. The reassembled-value traffic medium and\n   210\t  everything above it, `dao.stream.remote` included, is unchanged.\n   211\t\n   212\tThe host-free protocol tests (`test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc`) pass\n   213\tunchanged. The host seam tests are rewritten against section 3's seam,\n   214\tbecause the seam's signature changes from a function to invoke to a deposit\n   215\twriter.\n   216\t\n   217\t## 8. Exposure\n   218\t\n   219\tA raw writer handle entered in a mirror table\n   220\t(`dao.stream.remote.md` 2) lets whoever reaches it send arbitrary datagrams\n   221\tfrom this host. No composition does so without a gate on that entry.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba 'docs/design/dao.stream.remote.md'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoStream Remote: Any Stream, Reachable As Itself\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: design target, not implemented. Subordinate to\n     4\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), which is the contract and wins on any\n     5\tdisagreement. Every sentence below is a rule. It replaces the earlier serve\n     6\tdraft (git history, `docs/design/dao.stream.serve.md` at `71f3fb93`) and\n     7\tsucceeds `dao.jing.remote`, deprecated whole when this lands (plan section 1).\n     8\tSlices and companion edits are in\n     9\t[the implementation plan][impl-plan].\n    10\t\n    11\t## 1. Invariant, impossibilities, non-goals\n    12\t\n    13\tThe governing requirement, in the owner's words:\n    14\t\n    15\t> any implementation of dao.stream can be mechanically exposed via websocket\n    16\t> or udp and communicate p2p. client-server is just one stigmergic behavior\n    17\t> that interpreters implement. It should be able to traverse a NAT. A toy\n    18\t> example would be a string with a dao.stream wrapper that can be exposed on\n    19\t> a websocket on a remote interpreter.\n    20\t\n    21\t> There should be no concept of a server or client. its P2P but client/server\n    22\t> model can be implemented by convention. there is no priviledge server or\n    23\t> client.\n    24\t\n    25\t`dao.stream` is an abstraction boundary, not a network boundary. This\n    26\tdocument makes it a network boundary too, and keeps every network concept on\n    27\tits own side: nothing here adds an operation, an outcome or a key to the\n    28\tcontract beyond the five amendments listed in implementation plan section 2\n    29\t(OD-1, OD-2, OD-3, the `:dao.stream/refused` row, and the composed-handle\n    30\tsentence).\n    31\t\n    32\t**Impossibilities, stated plainly.**\n    33\t\n    34\t- Direct exchange needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n    35\t  coordinated simultaneous send. Where neither exists, a third peer with a\n    36\t  reachable address is required (section 4).\n    37\t- The unreachable cases are named by mapping and filtering behavior, not\n    38\t  product name: endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a\n    39\t  symmetric NAT; the strict paths of a carrier-grade NAT, which is address\n    40\t  sharing and not one behavior, since other carrier-grade paths hole-punch)\n    41\t  defeats punching, and relay is the workaround (section 4).\n    42\t- A browser cannot listen. It dials, or it is reached through a third peer.\n    43\t- Every peer needs an outbound path to a reachable WebSocket peer or relay.\n    44\t  If UDP is blocked and no such path exists, communication is impossible.\n    45\t- Everything a channel carries is readable by every peer that carries or\n    46\t  receives it until encryption is composed, and the encryption this version\n    47\t  specifies is value-level only: a relay still sees every envelope,\n    48\t  identities, ops, cursors, ids, sizes and timing included (section 7).\n    49\t- UDP messages are bounded by a composed maximum (section 3.2).\n    50\t- A reclaimed lease means the resource is gone, not disconnected (section 6).\n    51\t- A false lapse under partition is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n    52\t\n    53\t**Non-goals.** Authentication, authorization and key distribution (section 7\n    54\tnames the seam; `dao.shibi.md` owns the rest); channel-level encryption\n    55\t(deferred, section 7); WebRTC data channels; browser inbound; readiness\n    56\tnotification; exactly-once delivery.\n    57\t\n    58\t## 2. The design: mirror and reflection\n    59\t\n    60\tOne sentence: a peer that holds a handle answers the handle's own operations\n    61\tas data over a channel; a peer at the other end holds a handle whose\n    62\toperations are those questions asked across the channel, with the source's\n    63\toutcome maps returned verbatim. Four concepts.\n    64\t\n    65\t- **channel**: two ordinary handles with exactly two ends, a writer whose\n    66\t  `append!` carries one value toward the other end and a reader positioned\n    67\t  on the values the other end sent; built from a WebSocket attachment and\n    68\t  its projection (3.1), a UDP socket adapter and its deposit medium (3.2),\n    69\t  two ring buffers in one process, or two reflections served by a third\n    70\t  peer (3.3).\n    71\t- **table**: composition data on a peer, `{identity {:handle h :surface\n    72\t  S}}` plus the optional keys of sections 6 and 7. A plain map the\n    73\t  composition owns, not a registry (`dao.stream.md`, No hidden global\n    74\t  state).\n    75\t- **mirror step**: the pure step a peer runs over a channel to answer\n    76\t  requests against its table. It holds no state between calls beyond the\n    77\t  channel reader's cursor.\n    78\t- **reflection**: the handle `attach!` returns for a remote descriptor. A\n    79\t  **link**, one per channel, holds what the reflections on that channel\n    80\t  share.\n    81\t\n    82\tEvery peer may run any number of mirror steps and hold any number of\n    83\treflections, on the same channel, in both directions. No request names a\n    84\tkind of peer; the protocol has no peer identity at all. A peer is a channel\n    85\tend.\n    86\t\n    87\t### 2.1 Wire shapes\n    88\t\n    89\tTwo logical shapes cross every channel. Keys the contract owns stay under\n    90\t`:dao.stream/`; keys this document owns are under `:dao.stream.remote/`,\n    91\tthe transport-owned namespace rule of `dao.stream.md` (Envelopes).\n    92\t\n    93\t```clojure\n    94\t;; request\n    95\t{:dao.stream/identity        id\n    96\t :dao.stream.remote/op       op        ; :dao.stream/descriptor |\n    97\t                                       ; :dao.stream/cursor |\n    98\t                                       ; :dao.stream/next | :dao.stream/append!\n    99\t :dao.stream.remote/args     [...]     ; []  | [anchor] | [cursor] | [value]\n   100\t :dao.stream.remote/id       n         ; asker-minted, unique per channel\n   101\t                                       ; reader the asker holds\n   102\t :dao.stream.remote/budget   k}        ; optional, next only, a positive integer\n   103\t\n   104\t;; answer, the ordinary case: the contract's outcome map for the requested\n   105\t;; operation, verbatim, plus\n   106\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   107\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   108\t :dao.stream.remote/surface  S         ; descriptor answers only: the\n   109\t                                       ; entry's declared surface\n   110\t :dao.stream.remote/more     [...]}    ; optional, next only: further\n   111\t                                       ; outcome maps in order\n   112\t\n   113\t;; answer, a protocol error: one shape for every operation, a key instead of\n   114\t;; an outcome map, because no operation's outcome set can carry it\n   115\t{:dao.stream.remote/id       n\n   116\t :dao.stream/identity        id\n   117\t :dao.stream.remote/error    e}        ; one of the three errors below\n   118\t```\n   119\t\n   120\tA request is one value on the channel writer; an answer is one value on the\n   121\tother end's. The request map is open: keys not named here pass to the mirror\n   122\tstep's middleware chain (section 7) and are otherwise ignored. A value that\n   123\tis neither a well-formed request nor a well-formed answer is dropped as a\n   124\tdiagnostic below the mirror step and the link, as malformed wire input\n   125\t(`datom.world.md`, Host Boundaries). Wire ops are exactly four: `close!`\n   126\tnever crosses, because the mirror holds nothing per remote party to close\n   127\tand the owner's close is never remote; `attach!` never crosses, because it\n   128\tconsumes a descriptor and produces a local handle.\n   129\t\n   130\t**Protocol errors.** The mirror answers three errors with the error key,\n   131\tnever by fabricating an outcome the operation's closed set forbids\n   132\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention; Surfaces):\n   133\t\n   134\t- `not-found`: the identity is absent from the table, whether never served,\n   135\t  retired by policy, or reclaimed with its lease. An identity this peer does\n   136\t  not serve has no operations and no surface, so this one error is the\n   137\t  honest answer for all four ops.\n   138\t- `no-surface`: the op is outside the entry's declared surface, under the\n   139\t  fixed mapping: `cursor` and `next` need `:reader`, `append!` needs\n   140\t  `:writer`, `descriptor` needs no surface and is always answerable.\n   141\t- `oversize`: the channel writer refused the answer value itself\n   142\t  (`invalid-value`); the read is not skipped and no cursor advances past\n   143\t  it. Large media travel as `dao.jing` addresses (`dao.stream.md`,\n   144\t  Granularity).\n   145\t\n   146\tAll three names live under `:dao.stream.remote/`. The declared surface is\n   147\tanswer data, not descriptor data: the remote descriptor (2.2) carries no\n   148\tsurface, and the `descriptor` answer carries it under\n   149\t`:dao.stream.remote/surface`, the only place the surface is learned.\n   150\t\n   151\t### 2.2 The remote descriptor\n   152\t\n   153\t```clojure\n   154\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/remote\n   155\t :dao.stream/identity  id                      ; the served logical stream\n   156\t :dao.stream/channel   <channel descriptor>}   ; section 3: ws, udp or pair\n   157\t```\n   158\t\n   159\tThe descriptor names a stream and a channel. It carries no authorization, no\n   160\tsurface and no anchors; the mirror answers those. Two remote descriptors\n   161\tthat name one identity through different channels reach one stream, as the\n   162\tcontract's Envelopes section requires of descriptors for one logical stream\n   163\tserved by one transport (OD-3 (a), accepted). Attaching through this\n   164\ttransport never creates: a dynamic dispatch table has an\n   165\t`:dao.stream/attach` entry for `:dao.stream/remote` and no\n   166\t`:dao.stream/create` entry.\n   167\t\n   168\t### 2.3 The mirror step\n   169\t\n   170\t`(mirror-step table chan-reader cursor chan-writer) -> cursor'`. For each\n   171\trequest read from `chan-reader`, in order, bounded by a composition budget:\n   172\t\n   173\t1. Look up `:dao.stream/identity` in the table. Absent: append the answer\n   174\t   with `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/not-found`.\n   175\t2. Present, but the op outside the entry's declared surface under 2.1's\n   176\t   mapping: append the answer with\n   177\t   `:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/no-surface`.\n   178\t3. Otherwise answer `:dao.stream/descriptor` from the entry's handle\n   179\t   directly, adding `:dao.stream.remote/surface`; and apply\n   180\t   `:dao.stream/cursor`, `:dao.stream/next` and `:dao.stream/append!` to it\n   181\t   through `dao.stream.middleware/apply-request`\n   182\t   (`dao.stream.middleware.md`), with context\n   183\t   `{:dao.stream.remote/channel <channel attachment identity>}`. The budget\n   184\t   chase below runs the same `next` through the same path.\n   185\t4. Append the answer with `:dao.stream.remote/id` and\n   186\t   `:dao.stream/identity`.\n   187\t\n   188\tFor `:dao.stream/next` with a budget `k` the mirror may follow the successor\n   189\tcursor of each `ok` outcome up to `k - 1` further times and place the\n   190\tfurther outcome maps, in order, under `:dao.stream.remote/more`. The first\n   191\toutcome is the answer map itself. The vector ends at and includes the first\n   192\tnon-`ok` outcome when one occurs, so `blocked`, `end` and `gap` reach the\n   193\treader with the source's own recovery cursor. A mirror that ignores the\n   194\tbudget is correct. A value the channel cannot carry is answered with\n   195\t`:dao.stream.remote/error :dao.stream.remote/oversize` in place of that\n   196\telement (2.1).\n   197\t\n   198\tSteps 1 to 4 are the mirror's only contacts with the entry's handle: no\n   199\tanswer path calls a handle outside `apply-request`, so nothing bypasses the\n   200\tentry's middleware or its declared surface, and a reflection mints every\n   201\tanchor, `:oldest` and `:newest` included, by request.\n   202\t\n   203\tThe mirror never constructs, parses or rewrites a cursor, an anchor or an\n   204\toutcome. The served stream is the original: its cursors, its positions, its\n   205\t`gap`, its `end`. This is the whole basis of the claim in the title, and it\n   206\trests on cursors being plain data that survive the codec (OD-3 decision 2,\n   207\taccepted). A handle whose cursors hold a host object cannot be served, and\n   208\ta composition refuses to enter it into a table.\n   209\t\n   210\t### 2.4 The reflection\n   211\t\n   212\t`attach!` on a remote descriptor returns `:dao.stream/ok` at once with a\n   213\treflection handle and a local, opaque `:dao.stream/attachment`. This is the\n   214\tcontract's deferred remote confirmation (`dao.stream.md`, attach outcomes).\n   215\tThe host-composed attach closure keeps one link per channel descriptor,\n   216\tshared by every reflection through that channel. A link holds: the channel\n   217\twriter; the channel reader and the link's own cursor on it; the set of\n   218\toutstanding request ids with the request each was sent for; filed answers\n   219\tkeyed by id; an optional event writer; and the policy datum\n   220\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`.\n   221\t\n   222\tOn attach the link sends one `descriptor` request for the identity. Its\n   223\tanswer is the reflection's confirmation. The `not-found` error marks the\n   224\treflection **gone**; `ok` records the source's descriptor and declared\n   225\tsurface. Either is appended to the event writer when one is composed.\n   226\t\n   227\t**Drain.** Every operation on any reflection first reads the link's channel\n   228\treader to `blocked`, filing each answer whose id is outstanding, installing\n   229\teach `:dao.stream.remote/more` outcome at the cursor that precedes it, and\n   230\tfiling each protocol error under its id. Answers whose id is not\n   231\toutstanding are dropped. Non-answer values are dropped as diagnostics.\n   232\tThis is where the asynchrony goes (`dao.stream.md`, Where the asynchrony\n   233\tgoes): the caller's own polling is the cadence, and no driver step exists.\n   234\t\n   235\tThen the operation answers as follows.\n   236\t\n   237\t- `descriptor`: local. `ok` with the remote descriptor it was attached with\n   238\t  and `:dao.stream/identity`. Same identity as the source's own descriptor,\n   239\t  different reachability.\n   240\t- `cursor` `a`: a filed answer for `a` is returned, and a filed protocol\n   241\t  error is translated per the rules below. Otherwise a `cursor` request for\n   242\t  `a` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is `transport-error`\n   243\t  with `:dao.stream/retry? true`: the source's map, `invalid-anchor`\n   244\t  included, arrives later as an answer.\n   245\t- `next` `c`: a filed answer for `c` is returned and forgotten. Otherwise a\n   246\t  `next` request for `c` is sent if none is outstanding, and the answer is\n   247\t  `blocked`: nothing is observable at this position through this handle\n   248\t  yet.\n   249\t- `append!` `v`: send the request and return the channel writer's outcome.\n   250\t  `ok` is acceptance on the outbound path, exactly `dao.stream.md`\n   251\t  Writing's rule for a transport that carries values toward a stream\n   252\t  elsewhere. The source's outcome is appended to the event writer when it\n   253\t  is filed, correlated by id; without an event writer it is dropped.\n   254\t- `close!`: local. The reflection forgets its outstanding ids; afterwards\n   255\t  `append!` answers `closed`, `cursor` answers `closed`, `next` answers\n   256\t  filed outcomes then `end`. Nothing crosses the wire.\n   257\t\n   258\t**Protocol errors at the reflection.** A filed `not-found` marks the\n   259\treflection gone; once gone, every later `cursor`, `next` and `append!`\n   260\tanswers `transport-error` with\n   261\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found`, while\n   262\t`descriptor` still answers `ok`: a name outlives what it named. A filed\n   263\t`no-surface` or `oversize` marks nothing; each is returned, for the\n   264\toperation that caused it, as `transport-error` with\n   265\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason` set to the error's name. A reflection marked\n   266\tgone is how a remote holder observes reclaim (section 6). A request send\n   267\tthe channel writer refuses with `full` (establishment, back pressure)\n   268\tleaves the request unsent and the operation answers as though unanswered:\n   269\tthe retry cases above, `blocked` for `next`.\n   270\t\n   271\t**Channel loss.** A channel ends when its reader answers `end`: the\n   272\tprojection closed it (3.1), the pair adapter closed it on an `in` gap or\n   273\tend (3.3), or the medium is gone. The link then abandons its outstanding\n   274\tids and reports each\n   275\tabandoned `append!` on the event writer as `append-unknown` (2.5), and\n   276\tanswers later `cursor` and `next` `transport-error` with\n   277\t`:dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/channel-gone`, not retryable;\n   278\t`append!` keeps answering the channel writer's own outcome, `closed` once\n   279\tthe channel is down. Filed answers are still returned. Reattachment is the\n   280\tcaller's policy, by `attach!` on the same descriptor.\n   281\t\n   282\t**Refused.** A filed `:dao.stream/refused` is returned verbatim for the\n   283\toperation it answers and marks nothing: refusal is per operation\n   284\t(`dao.stream.md`, Result Convention).\n   285\t\n   286\t**Declared nature.** A reflection declares the surface its remote\n   287\tdescriptor's source declares plus `:closable`, learned from the attach\n   288\tprobe's answer (2.1); before that answer it declares\n   289\t`#{:reader :writer :closable}`, and an operation the source turns out to\n   290\tlack is answered as the `no-surface` case above. Its `blocked` is\n   291\thandle-relative, as the contract's Concurrency section already makes every\n   292\toutcome: one perspective at one moment. Its excluded outcomes: `next` never\n   293\tanswers `cursor-mismatch` or `invalid-cursor` from its own judgement, only\n   294\trelayed, and `append!` never answers the source's `full`, which arrives on\n   295\tthe event writer.\n   296\t\n   297\t### 2.5 Loss and resend\n   298\t\n   299\tThe reflection reads no clock. A link's `:dao.stream.remote/resend-after k`\n   300\tmeans: an outstanding `descriptor`, `cursor` or `next` request is re-sent\n   301\twhen the caller has asked `k` further times and it is still unanswered.\n   302\t`k` is unbounded on an ordered reliable channel and small on UDP. Those\n   303\tthree operations are idempotent, so a duplicate request recomputes the same\n   304\tor a later, equally true answer. `append!` is never re-sent:\n   305\tdeduplication and correlation are the payload's (OD-2, accepted), and an\n   306\tappend whose answer never arrives has unknown effect, reported on the\n   307\tevent writer as `{:dao.stream.remote/event :dao.stream.remote/append-unknown\n   308\t:dao.stream.remote/id n}` when the reflection is closed or the channel ends.\n   309\tThere are no sessions, no acknowledgement vectors, no windows and no result\n   310\tretention at the answering peer: the cursor is the sequence number for\n   311\treads, and `gap` remains the source's declaration, never a channel artifact.\n   312\t\n   313\t## 3. Channels\n   314\t\n   315\tA channel declares a portable value domain, a frame budget, and whether it\n   316\tis ordered and reliable; the link reads the last as composition data for\n   317\t`:dao.stream.remote/resend-after`, and nothing else in section 2 changes\n   318\tper channel.\n   319\t\n   320\t### 3.1 WebSocket\n   321\t\n   322\t`dao.stream.ws` as specified in `dao.stream.ws.md` is the socket layer, and\n   323\ta channel is one of its attachments plus a projection. **Reused**: the\n   324\tattachment model and identity; the adapter that deposits every event as\n   325\t`{:ws/attachment id :ws/event e :ws/value v}` on the medium the composition\n   326\twired; the writer handle, whose `append!` frames each value as one\n   327\t`{:ws/frame :ws/value :ws/value v}` message in the negotiated codec profile\n   328\t(Transit-JSON text or the CBOR binary profile); the bounded acceptance\n   329\thandoff with slot and acknowledgement; the resolution and lifecycle\n   330\tvocabulary. Ordered and reliable; frame budget unbounded.\n   331\t\n   332\t**New, the projection.** The mirror and the link speak raw request and\n   333\tanswer maps, so each channel end composes one step, `ws-project`, between\n   334\tthe ws deposit medium and the channel. The channel reader is a ring buffer\n   335\tthe composition wires; the channel writer is the ws handle itself.\n   336\t`ws-project` holds the reading cursor on the attachment's traffic medium,\n   337\tkeeps the events whose `:ws/attachment` names this channel, appends the\n   338\t`:ws/value` of each `:ws/payload` onto the ring buffer, and drops\n   339\t`:ws/error` diagnostics. A terminal lifecycle event for the attachment\n   340\t(`:ws/closed`, `:ws/ended`) or a failure resolution (`:ws/not-found`,\n   341\t`:ws/transport-error`) makes `ws-project` close the ring buffer: channel\n   342\tloss is then the link's `end` observation (2.4). `ws-project` is a step the\n   343\tcomposition drives at its own cadence, as it drives `endpoint-step`.\n   344\t\n   345\tDirection is establishment, not authority. A WebSocket has a dialer and an\n   346\tacceptor because TCP does. Once established the channel is symmetric and\n   347\tnothing in this document remembers who dialed. Hosts: clj, cljs on Node and\n   348\tcljd may dial or accept (`src/clj/dao/stream/ws/jvm.clj`,\n   349\t`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/node.cljs`, `src/cljd/dao/stream/ws/dart.cljd`);\n   350\tcljs in a browser may only dial (`src/cljs/dao/stream/ws/browser.cljs`).\n   351\t\n   352\t### 3.2 UDP\n   353\t\n   354\t`dao.stream.udp` is a new channel with the WebSocket deposit model: a socket\n   355\tretains nothing, so it has no reader surface, and its adapter deposits every\n   356\tdatagram as an event carrying the datagram's source address and its decoded\n   357\tvalue onto the medium the composition wired. The socket itself, as raw\n   358\taddressed datagrams, is the layer beneath this channel:\n   359\t[`dao.stream.datagram.md`](./dao.stream.datagram.md), whose section 7 says how\n   360\tthis channel is fed from it and whose `bind-host`/`bind-port` descriptor keys\n   361\tname the local socket, not the destination named here.\n   362\t\n   363\t- Descriptor: `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/udp :dao.stream/identity\n   364\t  <channel identity> :dao.stream.udp/host h :dao.stream.udp/port p}`.\n   365\t  `attach!` binds or reuses a local socket and returns a handle whose writer\n   366\t  path is datagrams to that address.\n   367\t- One CBOR value per datagram, canonical profile. The budget covers the\n   368\t  whole encoded datagram, fragment envelope included: at most 1200 bytes,\n   369\t  the figure `dao.jing.dht.node` already uses to clear common path MTUs.\n   370\t- **Fragmentation is channel-internal.** An encoded message over budget is\n   371\t  split into `k` datagrams, each\n   372\t  `{:dao.stream.remote/id n :dao.stream.udp/part i :dao.stream.udp/parts k\n   373\t  :dao.stream.udp/bytes b}`, where `n` is the carried message's own request\n   374\t  id. The receiver reassembles by `[channel attachment identity,\n   375\t  source-address, direction, id]`, because ids are unique only per channel\n   376\t  reader and one socket may serve several attachments, and delivers only a\n   377\t  complete message. Loss of any part is loss of the whole message,\n   378\t  recovered by the link's resend rule. Reassembly is bounded by two\n   379\t  composition data, `:dao.stream.udp/max-message-bytes` (default 64 KiB)\n   380\t  and `:dao.stream.udp/max-partial-messages`, evicting the oldest partial\n   381\t  message; no clock. Neither the mirror step nor the link sees a fragment.\n   382\t- Replies go to the datagram's source address, never to an address claimed\n   383\t  in a payload.\n   384\t- A send may name an explicit destination from an already-bound socket. Hole\n   385\t  punching depends on it (section 4).\n   386\t- Unordered and best-effort. Attachment identity is the remote host and port\n   387\t  as the socket saw them; it is the reflexive address a meeting peer\n   388\t  republishes.\n   389\t\n   390\tHosts: clj (`DatagramSocket`), cljs on Node (`dgram`), cljd\n   391\t(`RawDatagramSocket`). Browsers have no UDP.\n   392\t\n   393\t### 3.3 Pair\n   394\t\n   395\tA pair descriptor names two remote descriptors:\n   396\t\n   397\t```clojure\n   398\t{:dao.stream/type      :dao.stream/pair\n   399\t :dao.stream/identity  <channel identity>\n   400\t :dao.stream.remote/in  <remote descriptor>    ; this peer reads here\n   401\t :dao.stream.remote/out <remote descriptor>}   ; this peer writes here\n   402\t```\n   403\t\n   404\t`attach!` on it returns a channel whose reader is a reflection of `in` and\n   405\twhose writer is a reflection of `out`. When a third peer serves both\n   406\tstreams from its table, two peers that cannot reach each other have a\n   407\tchannel, and the third peer runs nothing but mirror steps over two ring\n   408\tbuffers; the requests and answers inside are values in its streams it never\n   409\tinterprets. Streams are values sent through streams. Ordered; best-effort\n   410\tunder the inboxes' retention. **An `in` gap ends the channel.** The pair\n   411\tadapter holds the reading cursor on `in`; when `next` there answers `gap`,\n   412\tframes were lost and the adapter cannot say which, so it closes the channel\n   413\treader for this link. The link observes `end` and runs its channel-loss\n   414\tpath (2.4): outstanding ids are abandoned, not re-sent, each abandoned\n   415\t`append!` is reported `append-unknown`, and reattachment is the caller's,\n   416\tby `attach!` on the same pair descriptor, which mints a fresh reading\n   417\tcursor on `in` at `:newest`. The gap is never reported to an inner reader\n   418\tas a source's `gap`. Double framing consumes budget: an inner value inside\n   419\tan outer `append!` must fit the outer channel's budget with both envelopes.\n   420\t\n   421\t## 4. Reachability and NAT\n   422\t\n   423\tA channel needs one end that can receive an unsolicited packet, or a\n   424\tcoordinated simultaneous send. The protocol cannot change that; it makes\n   425\tthe workarounds conventions. One peer, M below, is reachable and serves two\n   426\tordinary streams from its table; which peer that is carries no protocol\n   427\tmeaning, and any reachable peer can take its place.\n   428\t\n   429\t- One side receives unsolicited (public address, forwarded port, or\n   430\t  endpoint-independent mapping and filtering): direct; the other side dials,\n   431\t  WebSocket or UDP.\n   432\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping or filtering on one or both sides, punching\n   433\t  still possible: hole punch through M (below), UDP allowed.\n   434\t- Endpoint-dependent mapping and filtering on both sides (a symmetric NAT;\n   435\t  the strict carrier-grade paths): punching is unreliable; relay through a\n   436\t  pair served by M.\n   437\t- UDP blocked: WebSocket only, to a reachable peer, and the browser rule\n   438\t  applies; without one, impossible (section 1).\n   439\t- Browser at one end: it dials, and two browsers relay through a pair. A\n   440\t  WebRTC data channel is a future channel type whose candidates ride the\n   441\t  same meeting stream.\n   442\t\n   443\t**Hole punching, as a convention.** M serves a `meet-requests` stream with\n   444\tsurface `#{:writer}` and a `meet-board` stream with surface `#{:reader}`,\n   445\tboth ordinary table entries. Over a UDP channel a peer appends\n   446\t`{:meet/here <name>}`; M's interpreter reads the request and the channel\n   447\tattachment identity it arrived on, which for UDP is the reflexive address,\n   448\tand appends `{:meet/seen <name> :meet/reflexive {:host h :port p}\n   449\t:dao.lease/lease L}` to the board. Both parties of a call read the board,\n   450\tthen each sends a `descriptor` request with a fresh id to the other's\n   451\treflexive address from the same socket M observed. A path exists when an\n   452\tanswer, even a `not-found` error, carrying one of this peer's outstanding\n   453\tids arrives from the expected address; the first inbound datagram alone\n   454\tconfirms nothing. That is the whole of STUN, as facts on a stream; no probe\n   455\tframe exists.\n   456\t\n   457\t**Bounded meeting work.** M's interpreter bounds its own work: the board's\n   458\tretention (composition data, evict-oldest by default), the number of active\n   459\tpairs in its table, and the fanout of one driver step. A meeting request M\n   460\tcannot honor within its bounds is refused by a gate on its `meet-requests`\n   461\tentry and observed by the asker as `:dao.stream/refused` through its\n   462\treflection: exhaustion is a present answer, not silence. A peer reachable\n   463\tthrough M is reachable only while its channel to M lives, so its keep-alive\n   464\tcadence is bounded by the NAT binding's lifetime, tens of seconds for UDP,\n   465\tlonger for TCP.\n   466\t\n   467\t**Relay, as a convention.** A peer asks M, through `meet-requests`, for a\n   468\tpair; M creates two ring buffers, enters them in its table under a lease,\n   469\tand posts their remote descriptors on the board; the asking peers attach a\n   470\tpair channel over them (3.3). M interprets nothing it relays, and that two\n   471\tunreachable peers need M at all is a fact about the network, not a role in\n   472\tthis protocol. Reflexive addresses on a board are readable by every reader\n   473\tof the board; this convention claims no private delivery.\n   474\t\n   475\t## 5. Conventions that are not protocol\n   476\t\n   477\tNone of these has a frame. Each is peers using sections 2 and 3.\n   478\t\n   479\t**The toy.** Peer B holds a handle `s` over the single value `\"hello\"`,\n   480\tidentity `\"str-1\"`, complete history, surface `#{:reader}`. B's table is\n   481\t`{\"str-1\" {:handle s :surface #{:reader}}}`. B accepts WebSocket connections\n   482\tas `dao.stream.ws.md` specifies and drives, per accepted connection,\n   483\t`ws-project` and then `mirror-step` over the projected reader and the\n   484\tsocket handle. B publishes `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/remote\n   485\t:dao.stream/identity \"str-1\" :dao.stream/channel <B's ws descriptor>}`.\n   486\tPeer A calls `attach!`; the link dials and sends the `descriptor` probe;\n   487\tthe answer carries `:dao.stream.remote/surface #{:reader}`. A calls\n   488\t`cursor` with `:oldest`: no answer filed, the link sends the request and\n   489\tanswers `transport-error` with `:dao.stream/retry? true`; on the next call\n   490\tthe drain files the source's `ok` with `c0`. `next c0` proceeds the same\n   491\tway: `blocked`, then `ok \"hello\" c1`, and `next c1` is `end`. Nothing knew\n   492\tit was a string.\n   493\t\n   494\t**Request and response service.** A peer S enters `requests` with surface\n   495\t`#{:writer}` and `answers` with surface `#{:reader}` in its table and runs\n   496\tan interpreter over the local ends; a peer P attaches reflections to both,\n   497\tmints `:newest` on `answers` first, appends `{:svc/request r ...}` through\n   498\t`requests`, and reads `answers` until it observes `:svc/request r`. Request\n   499\tids are self-minted random values so callers sharing one `answers` stream\n   500\tdo not collide; a caller that needs private answers asks S for a pair. S is\n   501\ta \"server\" only in that its table has those entries and its interpreter has\n   502\tthat policy, and either peer may serve such a pair to the other over the\n   503\tsame channel. This is Linda's request medium, and the shape `yin.repl` and\n   504\tcontent lookup take (implementation plan, section 1). The meeting board and\n   505\tthe relay pair are section 4's instances of it.\n   506\t\n   507\t**Peer names.** A name is a self-certifying hash of a public key, the\n   508\tkickoff-hash form of `dao.stream.discovery.md`. It appears only inside\n   509\tconvention payloads. Nothing authenticates it until authentication exists.\n   510\t\n   511\t## 6. Lease integration\n   512\t\n   513\tA served stream is a network resource, so its lifetime beyond one call is\n   514\tgoverned by `dao.lease.md`. The judge is composed inside the peer that\n   515\tpossesses the resource, as that contract requires. What a lease governs:\n   516\t\n   517\t- A table entry a peer serves by its own policy: no lease; the peer retires\n   518\t  it when it likes.\n   519\t- A table entry served for a remote party (a stream a migrated continuation\n   520\t  still reads): yes; the remote party holds it, the holding peer grants and\n   521\t  judges, the identity is the subject.\n   522\t- A relay pair on a meeting peer: yes; the requesting peer holds it, the\n   523\t  meeting peer grants and judges, the two identities are the subject.\n   524\t- Link state, UDP reassembly buffers, channel sockets: no; governed by close\n   525\t  or by a bounded buffer.\n   526\t- NAT mappings and punch state: no; possessed by the NAT box, so no judge\n   527\t  can exist, and keep-alive cadence is the peer's own (section 4).\n   528\t- Board postings: no; records under the board's retention, each carrying the\n   529\t  lease id of the registration or pair it belongs to, and a reader treats a\n   530\t  posting whose lease has a `:lapsed` on the board as stale.\n   531\t\n   532\t**Mechanism: no new wire shape.** A grantor enters two conventional streams\n   533\tin its table, `lease-proposals` with surface `#{:writer}` and\n   534\t`lease-grants` with surface `#{:reader}`, published as remote descriptors\n   535\tbeside its meeting descriptors. A holder appends a proposal through a\n   536\treflection on the first and reads its grant through a reflection on the\n   537\tsecond. The grant creates one more table entry, a ring buffer with surface\n   538\t`#{:writer}`, and names its identity as `:dao.lease/holder`: this is the\n   539\tholder's **renewal medium**, and whatever lands on it is attributed to the\n   540\tholder by the medium, the attribution resolver `dao.lease.md` names as\n   541\tper-author media. The judge reads it with a local cursor. The renewal\n   542\tmedium lives and dies with its lease; the reclaim procedure removes the\n   543\tsubject entries and the renewal entry together, idempotently, and reports\n   544\tsuccess. A table entry under lease carries `:dao.lease/lease L`.\n   545\t\n   546\tUntil ShiBi exists, a renewal medium is reachable by anyone who learns its\n   547\tdescriptor and accepts a third party's renewal, extending a lease it does\n   548\tnot hold: attribution needs no credential, extension may want one, and a\n   549\tcomposition that must not accept it composes a gate on the renewal entry.\n   550\t\n   551\t**Renewal.** `append!` of `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal\n   552\t:dao.lease/lease L}` on the holder's reflection of its renewal medium. The\n   553\treflection's `ok` is outbound acceptance only; the holder advances its\n   554\trenewal bound only on the source's `ok` observed on the link's event\n   555\twriter. A holder with no event writer stops at its bound early, never late.\n   556\t\n   557\t**Reclaim observed.** The mirror answers the `not-found` protocol error for\n   558\ta reclaimed identity and the reflection marks itself gone (2.4). The holder\n   559\tmay also read the grantor's own `:lapsed` record through its reflection on\n   560\t`lease-grants`: that is observing the grantor's stream, not carriage. And\n   561\tthe holder stops at its own bound regardless.\n   562\t\n   563\t**Expiry versus channel loss.** Channel loss is the absence of answers or\n   564\tthe channel's `end` (2.4); the table entry persists, so a holder that\n   565\treconnects within duration plus tolerance finds its entry, its cursors and\n   566\tits lease intact. Expiry is a present answer, the `not-found` error, on a\n   567\thealthy channel. Only expiry frees anything. A meeting peer that restarts\n   568\tloses its table and ledger, so every pair is gone, holders observe\n   569\t`not-found` and re-propose; this is `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant\n   570\twith an empty inventory, correct because the table is process-scoped.\n   571\t\n   572\tUnits are the composition's; these conventions fix `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`.\n   573\t\n   574\t## 7. Middleware and the ShiBi seam\n   575\t\n   576\tMiddleware preserves positions and outcome kinds as specified in\n   577\t`dao.stream.middleware.md`. Network attachment points are the table entry\n   578\t(gate, value encryption, redaction or metering), the reflection (credential\n   579\tpresentation, value decryption or metering), and the channel (reserved for\n   580\tdeferred channel-level wrapping).\n   581\t\n   582\tKeys and chains are composition data, never descriptor fields. Value-level\n   583\tencryption hides values only; identity, ops, cursors, sizes and timing stay\n   584\tvisible. Channel encryption is deferred: a relay sees every envelope and\n   585\tany value not hidden by middleware. A future channel wrapper must order\n   586\tcodec, encryption and fragmentation in that order. Key distribution is\n   587\tunspecified; use library ciphers.\n   588\t\n   589\tThe capability-agnostic seam is an opaque per-request credential key,\n   590\tmirror-supplied channel context, mirror-side gate and reflection-side\n   591\t`present`; refusal is `:dao.stream/refused`. No token or verification\n   592\tpolicy is specified. The gate consumes an index-published decision. The\n   593\tone ShiBi fit this version chooses: its index interpreter publishes the\n   594\ttuple space's current query value as the decision, and its query\n   595\tinterpreter is `verify`, a query over that value with the request as\n   596\tbindings, the shape `dao.space.query/q` has. `dao.shibi.md` states the\n   597\tadapter contract and its limits (one snapshot, bounded cost, no waiting);\n   598\trequest-specific decisions awaited over streams are a non-goal there.\n   599\t\n   600\tLease attribution uses per-author media; renewal gates are optional.\n   601\t\n   602\t## 8. Unification and contract boundary\n   603\t\n   604\t`dao.jing.content` succeeds `dao.jing.remote` whole. The implementation\n   605\tplan lists the successor vocabulary (section 1), the network-path fates\n   606\t(section 5) and the five `dao.stream.md` amendments (section 2), which add\n   607\tno network or capability concept.\n   608\t\n   609\t[impl-plan]: ./dao.stream.remote.implementation-plan.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba 'docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# DaoJing DHT: Content-Addressed Segment Distribution\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: **contract frozen 2026-09-30 (DHT epic slice S0); sections 2 to 9 are\n     4\tthe design target, not yet implemented.** Section 10 records what the source\n     5\ttree holds today and what each slice deletes. Subordinate to\n     6\t[`dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md), [`dao.jing.md`](./dao.jing.md) and\n     7\t[`datom.world.md`](./datom.world.md). In sections 2 to 9 every sentence is a\n     8\trule.\n     9\t\n    10\t## 1. Overview and scope\n    11\t\n    12\t`dao.jing` is deliberately dumb: it observes an explicit intake pool of\n    13\t`dao.stream` values and materializes opaque payloads into content-addressed\n    14\tstorage (see `dao.jing.md`). This document specifies the distributed\n    15\tcontent-store backend for that boundary: a Kademlia-style Distributed Hash\n    16\tTable that routes only strict content addresses.\n    17\t\n    18\tThe DHT answers one question: given a content address, where on the network\n    19\tare the bytes? It does nothing else. There are no roots, no CAS records, no\n    20\tdeletes, no source identity, no stream replication, and no consensus. Content\n    21\taddressing stays in `dao.jing` (`segment-key`); the network layer only\n    22\tdistributes the payloads those addresses name.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Strict content addresses only.** The DHT routes every registered\n    25\t`:segment/<algorithm>-<digest>` address of the closed multihash registry\n    26\t(`dao.jing.hash-registry.md`; `:blake3` default, `:sha256` selectable), and\n    27\tnothing else. The routing target is the address's digest alone; a non-segment\n    28\taddress is rejected before any local or network action. Because the payload\n    29\thashes to the address, a fetching node verifies what a peer hands it against\n    30\tthe address it asked for.\n    31\t\n    32\tNothing at this layer knows or records which stream, agent, or peer carried a\n    33\tpayload. Distribution is by content, never by provenance.\n    34\t\n    35\t**Root discovery is outside this epic (owner decision 3).** A reader holding\n    36\tonly a peer address cannot learn which manifest is current. A restart reads\n    37\tthe local HEAD of the durable-store epic; a remote query is given a manifest\n    38\taddress. Signed root facts and rendezvous are a separate later epic.\n    39\t\n    40\t## 2. Layering and composition\n    41\t\n    42\tThe governing direction, in the owner's words: \"the dht should sit on the raw\n    43\tdatagram layer but expose a dao.stream interface\"; \"Both — A raw datagram\n    44\tlayer exposed as dao.stream, AND a DHT whose own API is dao.stream-shaped,\n    45\tbuilt on it.\"\n    46\t\n    47\tThe DHT is one interpreter, `dao.jing.dht`, between two stream-shaped\n    48\tboundaries. Below it is one raw datagram socket\n    49\t([`dao.stream.datagram.md`](./dao.stream.datagram.md)). Above it is the\n    50\tcontent request medium `dao.jing.content` already defines. It is a pure\n    51\tstepped state machine: no socket, thread, atom, promise, callback, scheduler\n    52\tor clock, on any host.\n    53\t\n    54\tThe composition supplies, as data:\n    55\t\n    56\t| Datum | Meaning |\n    57\t|---|---|\n    58\t| `:local` | A `dao.jing` byte-store handle: this node's own copy of what it holds. |\n    59\t| `:requests` + cursor | Reader on the request stream (section 3). An evicting ring. |\n    60\t| `:answers` | Writer for the exact `:jing/*` answers (section 3). An evicting ring. |\n    61\t| `:facts` | Writer for DHT-qualified facts (section 3). An evicting ring. |\n    62\t| `:ticks` + cursor | Reader on a tick stream (section 5). |\n    63\t| `:traffic` + cursor, `:datagrams` | Reader on the socket's raw traffic stream and its writer handle. **Both absent in solo mode.** |\n    64\t| `:dao.jing.dht/id` | This node's id: 64 lowercase hex characters, 32 bytes of the key space (section 6). |\n    65\t| `:dao.jing.dht/secret` | Root secret bytes for cookies (section 8). Absent in solo mode. |\n    66\t| `:dao.jing.dht/bootstrap` | Contacts `[{:host ip-literal :port p} ...]`: unproven candidates, nothing more. |\n    67\t| `:dao.jing.dht/publish?` | Publication declaration, default `false` (section 4.4). |\n    68\t| `:dao.jing.dht/ack-peers` | Peers a write must be sent to before it is acknowledged. Default 2, floor 2, ceiling `k` (section 4). |\n    69\t| limits and tick budgets | Section 9. |\n    70\t\n    71\t`(dao.jing.dht/state composition) -> state` validates the composition and\n    72\tthrows on a composition defect (an `ack-peers` below 2 or above `k`, a missing\n    73\thandle, a socket without a secret). It performs no stream operation.\n    74\t`(dao.jing.dht/step state budget) -> state'` is the only thing that advances\n    75\tthe DHT. One owner per state drives it, at the composition's cadence. The\n    76\tstate is a plain map: handles, cursors, the routing table, and the pending\n    77\ttables.\n    78\t\n    79\t**Solo mode** is a composition with no `:traffic` and no `:datagrams`. It\n    80\topens no socket, holds no pending network work, and answers as section 4.3\n    81\tand 4.5 say. Configuring peers later is a new composition.\n    82\t\n    83\tOne step, in this order, each stage bounded by `budget`:\n    84\t\n    85\t1. Drain `:ticks` to `blocked`; the newest reading is `now`.\n    86\t2. Read `:traffic`: validate each datagram (section 7, 8), deliver replies to\n    87\t   pending queries, serve requests.\n    88\t3. Read `:requests`.\n    89\t4. Advance pending work: expire deadlines against `now`, issue queries,\n    90\t   retries and sends.\n    91\t5. Append answers and facts.\n    92\t\n    93\tA `gap` on `:requests`, `:traffic` or `:ticks` adopts the recovery cursor and\n    94\tappends `{:dao.jing.dht/fact :dao.jing.dht/gap :dao.jing.dht/stream s}`. A\n    95\trequest lost to a gap is never answered; its asker re-asks. Every operation\n    96\there is idempotent by content address, so re-asking is always safe.\n    97\t\n    98\t## 3. The stream API\n    99\t\n   100\t**Requests and answers are the `dao.jing.content` convention**\n   101\t(`src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc`), unchanged and exact. The DHT is one more\n   102\tserving half of that convention, a sibling of `serve-step` whose answers may\n   103\tarrive on a later step. The clients are the existing ones:\n   104\t`dao.jing.content.step` (portable, stepped), `dao.jing.content.driver` (JVM\n   105\tblocking, host policy), `dao.jing.content.async` (callback facade). No DHT\n   106\tclient vocabulary exists.\n   107\t\n   108\t```clojure\n   109\t;; requests\n   110\t{:jing/request r :jing/get address}\n   111\t{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}\n   112\t{:dao.jing.dht/replicate address}            ; section 4.2; no id, the address is the identity\n   113\t\n   114\t;; answers: exact key sets, no additional keys\n   115\t{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64-or-nil}\n   116\t{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}\n   117\t{:jing/request r :jing/unacknowledged reason}     ; the one addition, section 4.3\n   118\t```\n   119\t\n   120\t- `r` is self-minted and random, as `dao.jing.content` requires. The DHT keeps\n   121\t  no record of completed ids and refuses no duplicate: correlation and\n   122\t  deduplication are the payload's (`dao.stream.md`, Writing).\n   123\t- Answers carry exactly the keys shown, because the stepped client matches\n   124\t  exact key sets. Everything DHT-specific goes on `:facts`.\n   125\t- A malformed request is dropped without answer, as `serve-step` does.\n   126\t- `:jing/unacknowledged` is one new exact answer shape in the `:jing/*`\n   127\t  convention. `dao.jing.content.step` completes it as\n   128\t  `{:id id :error {:code :dao.jing.content/unacknowledged :reason reason}}`.\n   129\t  A purely local content service never produces it.\n   130\t\n   131\t**Facts** are plain data under `:dao.jing.dht/`, one map per fact, keyed by\n   132\taddress, for whoever reads them:\n   133\t\n   134\t| `:dao.jing.dht/fact` | Also carries | Meaning |\n   135\t|---|---|---|\n   136\t| `:dao.jing.dht/sent` | `/address`, `/peers n` | **The acknowledgement.** Every datagram of the write was handed to the socket toward `n` distinct peers, `n >= ack-peers`. |\n   137\t| `:dao.jing.dht/unacknowledged` | `/address`, `/reason`, `/peers m`, `/local` | The write is not acknowledged. `m < ack-peers` is how many peers it was sent to; `/local` is the local verdict or `nil`. |\n   138\t| `:dao.jing.dht/replicated` | `/address`, `/peers n`, `/confirmed c` | Replication after the acknowledgement has finished. `c` peers replied that they stored it. |\n   139\t| `:dao.jing.dht/miss` | `/address`, `/reason` | A get ended not found: `/exhausted`, `/deadline`, `/solo`, `/busy`. |\n   140\t| `:dao.jing.dht/gap` | `/stream` | Section 2. |\n   141\t\n   142\t`:dao.stream/*` operation outcomes are untouched: these are values on\n   143\tstreams, not outcomes of stream operations.\n   144\t\n   145\t## 4. Writes: local insert, then acknowledged when sent to multiple peers\n   146\t\n   147\tThe owner's contract (2026-09-30), verbatim: \"i want to take the kafka route.\n   148\twrite is acknowledged immediately as long as it is sent out on a network. this\n   149\tworks because the data is written to a peer\"; \"sent to a peer but it must send\n   150\tto multiple peers before it can be considered ack\"; local copy: \"Yes, local +\n   151\tnetwork\". This supersedes the earlier local-verdict acknowledgement.\n   152\t\n   153\t### 4.1 What the acknowledgement asserts\n   154\t\n   155\t**A write is acknowledged when the local insert has succeeded and every\n   156\tdatagram of its store message has been handed to the socket toward at least\n   157\t`ack-peers` distinct peers.**\n   158\t\n   159\t- \"Handed to the socket\" is the raw writer's `append!` answering\n   160\t  `:dao.stream/ok` (`dao.stream.datagram.md` section 4), for every datagram of\n   161\t  the message, chunks included. A peer for which any datagram answered\n   162\t  otherwise is not counted, and the next candidate is tried.\n   163\t- A peer counts only if it is a **proven** routing entry (section 6) that is\n   164\t  **fresh**: a matched reply from it arrived within the last\n   165\t  `cookie-epoch-ticks` by this node's own ticks. That reply is also what\n   166\t  carried the cookie the store uses (section 8). A peer that is not fresh is\n   167\t  pinged first.\n   168\t- The acknowledgement says **sent to N peers**. It never says stored by N\n   169\t  peers, received by N peers, or durable anywhere but locally. The word\n   170\t  \"stored\" appears only in `:dao.jing.dht/confirmed`, and that number is\n   171\t  peers' own replies, which are claims.\n   172\t- `ack-peers` is composition data: default 2, floor 2, ceiling `k`. One peer\n   173\t  is never \"multiple\".\n   174\t- A local collision or an invalid address/bytes pair fails before anything\n   175\t  else, as today.\n   176\t\n   177\t### 4.2 Two entry points, one pending write\n   178\t\n   179\t- **The byte-store handle** (section 5.1). `put-bytes-fn` validates, inserts\n   180\t  into `:local`, appends `{:dao.jing.dht/replicate address}` to the request\n   181\t  stream, and returns the local verdict `:inserted` or `:present` at once.\n   182\t  The return value is the byte-store contract's local verdict. It is **not**\n   183\t  the acknowledgement; the acknowledgement is the `/sent` fact.\n   184\t- **A `:jing/put` request.** The step validates (the one ingress check,\n   185\t  `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`), inserts into `:local`, and holds the answer.\n   186\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result verdict}` is appended when the write is\n   187\t  acknowledged, and in a DHT composition that answer **is** the\n   188\t  acknowledgement.\n   189\t\n   190\tBoth create or join one pending write per address. A pending write records\n   191\tthe address, the request ids waiting on it, the peers sent to, and its\n   192\tdeadline. It never holds the payload; bytes are read from `:local` when sent.\n   193\t\n   194\t### 4.3 How a write proceeds, and what the caller sees when it cannot\n   195\t\n   196\tA pending write never waits on a lookup to acknowledge:\n   197\t\n   198\t1. **Candidates** are the proven routing entries nearest the address's digest\n   199\t   that this node already knows: a table read, no IO.\n   200\t2. **Send** the store message to fresh candidates, nearest first, until `ack-peers` are counted. With a warm table this happens in the\n   201\t   step that read the request: the acknowledgement is immediate.\n   202\t3. **Too few known peers** (a cold bootstrap, a small table): the write starts\n   203\t   an iterative lookup toward the digest and pings candidates; each newly\n   204\t   proven peer is sent to as it appears.\n   205\t4. **Acknowledge** the moment the count reaches `ack-peers`: append the\n   206\t   `/sent` fact and answer every waiting `:jing/put`.\n   207\t5. **Continue after the acknowledgement**, best effort: finish the lookup and\n   208\t   send to the `k` nearest peers not already sent to, so that a reader's\n   209\t   lookup converges on holders. When done or at the deadline, append\n   210\t   `/replicated`. Nothing about the acknowledgement depends on this.\n   211\t\n   212\tIf the count has not reached `ack-peers` when `ack-ticks` have elapsed since\n   213\tthe write began, the write is **not acknowledged**: append the\n   214\t`/unacknowledged` fact and answer every waiting `:jing/put` with\n   215\t`{:jing/request r :jing/unacknowledged reason}`. The pending write is then\n   216\tforgotten. The local copy stays. Trying again is the caller's policy: append\n   217\tthe replicate request again.\n   218\t\n   219\t| Reason | When | Sent anything? |\n   220\t|---|---|---|\n   221\t| `:dao.jing.dht/solo` | No socket is composed. Answered in the step that reads the request. | No |\n   222\t| `:dao.jing.dht/unpublished` | `publish?` is false (section 4.4). Answered at once. | No |\n   223\t| `:dao.jing.dht/oversize` | The encoded store message exceeds `max-message-bytes`. Answered at once. | No |\n   224\t| `:dao.jing.dht/absent` | A replicate request names an address `:local` does not hold. | No |\n   225\t| `:dao.jing.dht/busy` | `max-pending-writes` is reached. Answered at once. | No |\n   226\t| `:dao.jing.dht/too-few-peers` | The deadline passed with fewer than `ack-peers` counted. `/peers m` says how many. | Possibly, to `m` |\n   227\t\n   228\tSo a caller always sees exactly one of `/sent` or `/unacknowledged` per\n   229\tpending write, unless the request itself was lost to a gap, which the `/gap`\n   230\tfact reports.\n   231\t\n   232\t**Oversize fails explicitly and before the local insert.** A payload whose\n   233\tstore message would exceed `max-message-bytes` is rejected by `put-bytes-fn`\n   234\t(it throws, as for any invalid put) and by the `:jing/put` path (the\n   235\t`/oversize` answer, no local insert), in every mode including solo. A segment\n   236\tis never silently local-only.\n   237\t\n   238\t**No REPL round stalls.** `put-bytes-fn` returns after the local insert and\n   239\tone ring append. A round that publishes and reads its manifest back\n   240\t(`yin.repl.index`) completes against `:local`. The acknowledgement arrives on\n   241\t`:facts` when the runner has sent; the REPL reports it when it observes it.\n   242\t\n   243\t### 4.4 Publication (owner decision 1)\n   244\t\n   245\tDefault: never publish. `:dao.jing.dht/publish?` is a separate declaration,\n   246\tdistinct from configuring peers; bootstrap contacts alone declare nothing.\n   247\t\n   248\t- **`publish?` false:** this node originates nothing and serves nothing it\n   249\t  holds. Every write is `/unpublished`. An inbound `:fetch` is answered not\n   250\t  found; an inbound `:store` is answered not ok. The node still answers\n   251\t  `:ping` and `:find`, and still fetches and caches what it asks for.\n   252\t- **`publish?` true:** everything in `:local` is public. The node replicates\n   253\t  its writes, serves `:fetch` from `:local`, and accepts `:store` within its\n   254\t  storage bound. The composition that sets it states what will be shared: the\n   255\t  whole content of that store.\n   256\t\n   257\tThis is a declassification decision, not authorization. ShiBi is where\n   258\tauthorization arrives.\n   259\t\n   260\t### 4.5 Reads\n   261\t\n   262\tA `:jing/get` request reads `:local` first and answers a hit in the same\n   263\tstep. On a miss:\n   264\t\n   265\t- Solo: answer not found, with a `/miss` fact of reason `/solo`.\n   266\t- Otherwise start a pending get: an iterative lookup toward the digest, asking\n   267\t  candidates with `:fetch` as they are proven, at most `alpha` outstanding.\n   268\t- A fetched payload is verified before anything else: strict decode, digest\n   269\t  against the requested address, canonical decode (`dao.jing/accept-bytes!`).\n   270\t  A failure is discarded and the next candidate tried.\n   271\t- A verified payload is inserted into `:local`, then answered found.\n   272\t- Candidates exhausted, or `get-ticks` elapsed: answer not found, with a\n   273\t  `/miss` fact.\n   274\t\n   275\t**Not found from a DHT is not authoritative absence.** It says no reachable\n   276\tcandidate produced a verifiable value in time.\n   277\t\n   278\t## 5. The `dao.jing` adapter, the JVM facade, and time\n   279\t\n   280\t### 5.1 The byte-store handle (owner decision 4)\n   281\t\n   282\t`(dao.jing.dht/store-handle {:local l :requests w :max-message-bytes n})` is\n   283\ta plain-data `dao.jing` handle, portable to every host:\n   284\t\n   285\t- `put-bytes-fn`: section 4.2. Local verdict, immediately.\n   286\t- `get-bytes-fn`: **`:local` only.** Bytes or the caller's sentinel. It never\n   287\t  waits, never returns a marker, and never touches the network.\n   288\t- `close-fn`: closes `:local`.\n   289\t\n   290\tIt works with `dao.jing/materialize!`, `dao.jing/get` and `dao.jing/close!`,\n   291\tand is a valid handle for `dao.jing.content/serve-step`.\n   292\t\n   293\t**A remote miss is staged around the handle, not inside it.** The\n   294\tcomposition issues `:jing/get` through `dao.jing.content.step`, drives the\n   295\tDHT step until the completion lands (the verified bytes are then in\n   296\t`:local`), and reads through the handle again. `dao.data.btree.storage`'s\n   297\thydration cache over `dao.jing.content.async` is the existing consumer shape.\n   298\t\n   299\tThis changes today's behavior, where a DHT `get` fetched synchronously on a\n   300\tmiss. The owner ratified the change.\n   301\t\n   302\t### 5.2 The JVM blocking facade\n   303\t\n   304\tHost policy, JVM only, optional. It owns a driver thread that is the DHT\n   305\tstate's one step owner and the tick adapter's cadence, and it wraps\n   306\t`dao.jing.content.driver` so that a `get-bytes-fn` miss issues `:jing/get`\n   307\tand waits to the driver's request deadline. Its `put-bytes-fn` is 5.1's: it\n   308\treturns the local verdict and does not wait for the acknowledgement. No\n   309\tportable code is written against the facade.\n   310\t\n   311\t### 5.3 Time\n   312\t\n   313\tTime reaches the DHT only as data. The step reads a tick stream the\n   314\tcomposition wires and never a host clock, exactly as `dao.lease.md` (Time)\n   315\trequires of lease code, and it reads the same fact:\n   316\t`{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/tick :dao.lease/reading n}`. Readings never\n   317\tdecrease. Units are the composition's; this contract fixes `{:ms 1}`.\n   318\t\n   319\tEvery duration in this document is a tick difference: query deadlines,\n   320\t`ack-ticks`, `get-ticks`, partial-message eviction, cookie epochs. A step\n   321\tthat has observed no tick expires nothing. A tick gap makes expiry late, not\n   322\twrong. Tests drive time by appending ticks.\n   323\t\n   324\t## 6. Peers and routing\n   325\t\n   326\t- **A node id is not derived from an address.** It is 32 bytes of the key\n   327\t  space, composition data. The recommended form is the self-certifying name\n   328\t  of `dao.stream.remote.md` section 5 (a hash of a public key). Until\n   329\t  authentication exists nothing verifies an id, and a peer may claim any id;\n   330\t  that limit is stated, not hidden.\n   331\t- **A routing entry is `{:id id :host h :port p}` where `h` and `p` are the\n   332\t  address the socket observed**, never an address a payload claims. No wire\n   333\t  message carries the sender's own address.\n   334\t- **An entry is recorded only when proven returnable:** a reply arrives from\n   335\t  the expected observed address matching a pending query, or a request\n   336\t  arrives carrying a valid cookie. Nothing is recorded from an unvalidated\n   337\t  datagram.\n   338\t- Peers listed in a `:find` reply are third-party **hints**: candidates to\n   339\t  ping, never entries.\n   340\t- Replies go to the datagram's observed source.\n   341\t- A proven entry whose id reappears from a different proven address is\n   342\t  replaced: a NAT rebinding looks exactly like that.\n   343\t- The table is `dao.jing.dht.kad`: `k = 20`, XOR distance, least-recently-seen\n   344\t  eviction.\n   345\t- **Lookup is explicit state**: target, shortlist, queried set, outstanding\n   346\t  queries (at most `alpha = 3`). A candidate whose query exhausted its tries\n   347\t  is dead for that lookup and is removed from the table, and the shortlist is\n   348\t  refilled with the next-nearest candidate. A lookup ends when `k` peers have\n   349\t  answered or candidates are exhausted.\n   350\t\n   351\tNAT meeting is not in this epic. The DHT may share a hole-punched socket with\n   352\t`dao.stream.udp` (`dao.stream.datagram.md` section 6), and the identity and\n   353\taddress rules above are what keep wire v1 usable once meeting exists.\n   354\t\n   355\t## 7. The wire (v1)\n   356\t\n   357\tOne CBOR value per datagram, encoded with `dao.stream.cbor` (the stream\n   358\tcodec, canonical profile, available on every host). Not `dao.jing.cbor`, and\n   359\tnot Transit. This is a clean break from today's wire; no fleet exists.\n   360\t\n   361\t```clojure\n   362\t;; request\n   363\t{:dao.jing.dht/v 1\n   364\t :op      :ping | :find | :store | :fetch\n   365\t :q       n                  ; query id: self-minted random safe integer\n   366\t :id      <32 bytes>         ; sender's node id\n   367\t :cookie  <bytes>            ; the cookie this peer last gave the sender; absent on first contact\n   368\t :target  <32 bytes>         ; :find\n   369\t :address a                  ; :store, :fetch\n   370\t :bytes   <byte string>}     ; :store: the canonical payload bytes\n   371\t\n   372\t;; reply\n   373\t{:dao.jing.dht/v 1\n   374\t :op      :reply\n   375\t :q       n\n   376\t :id      <32 bytes>\n   377\t :cookie  <bytes>            ; a cookie for the requester's observed address\n   378\t ;; then exactly one of:\n   379\t :need-cookie true\n   380\t :ok      boolean            ; :ping, :store\n   381\t :peers   [[<32 bytes> host port] ...]   ; :find\n   382\t :found   boolean :bytes <byte string>}  ; :fetch\n   383\t\n   384\t;; chunk\n   385\t{:dao.jing.dht/v 1\n   386\t :op :chunk :q n :dir :request | :reply\n   387\t :part i :parts k\n   388\t :cookie <bytes>             ; :dir :request only\n   389\t :bytes  <byte string>}\n   390\t```\n   391\t\n   392\t- `:dao.jing.dht/v` is the discriminator on a shared socket and the version.\n   393\t  A datagram that does not decode to a map carrying it is not the DHT's and is\n   394\t  dropped silently. An unknown version is dropped without reply.\n   395\t- Payload and chunk bytes are CBOR byte strings on the wire. Base64 appears\n   396\t  only in stream-visible values (`:jing/bytes`, raw datagram events).\n   397\t- Ids and targets are raw 32-byte strings on the wire and hex in state.\n   398\t- **Peers per `:find` reply: at most 8**, as `[id host port]` tuples. The\n   399\t  iterative lookup asks again for more. S3 proves by test that the largest\n   400\t  legal reply (8 peers, IPv6 literals) encodes within `max-datagram`.\n   401\t- **A reply is matched by `[observed source, :q]`** against a pending query\n   402\t  sent to that address. `:q` alone matches nothing.\n   403\t- **Chunks.** A message whose encoding exceeds the datagram budget is sent as\n   404\t  `:chunk` records, each fitting the budget. Reassembly is keyed\n   405\t  `[observed host, observed port, :dir, :q]`. Duplicates are ignored;\n   406\t  a `:parts` disagreeing with the held count is dropped; accumulated bytes\n   407\t  over `max-message-bytes` drop the partial; incomplete partials are evicted\n   408\t  oldest-first past `max-partial-messages` and by age in ticks. Loss of a part\n   409\t  is loss of the message, recovered by retrying the whole query.\n   410\t- The total encoded size is checked against `max-message-bytes` **before any\n   411\t  chunk is sent**.\n   412\t- The DHT does not use `dao.stream.udp`'s fragment envelope or its\n   413\t  `:dao.stream.remote/id` identity.\n   414\t- **Reliability is the requester's**: each query has a deadline of\n   415\t  `query-ticks` and `tries` attempts, then the peer is unreachable for that\n   416\t  operation.\n   417\t\n   418\t## 8. Hardening\n   419\t\n   420\tImplemented where the DHT reads raw events, never in the raw layer. Non-loopback\n   421\texposure waits for all of it (slice S4).\n   422\t\n   423\t- **Cookie.** `cookie = MAC(epoch-secret, observed-host | observed-port)`,\n   424\t  truncated to 16 bytes. `epoch-secret = MAC(:dao.jing.dht/secret, epoch)`,\n   425\t  `epoch = floor(now / cookie-epoch-ticks)`. A cookie is valid under the\n   426\t  current or the previous epoch. Verification is stateless. The MAC is\n   427\t  HMAC-SHA-256 from each host's library; nothing is hand-rolled.\n   428\t- **Without a valid cookie a request gets one reply: `:need-cookie` plus a\n   429\t  cookie, no larger than the request datagram.** No work, no lookup, no\n   430\t  storage, no reassembly. The requester retries with the cookie; that retry\n   431\t  does not consume a try.\n   432\t- **A request chunk is verified before any partial state is allocated.** A\n   433\t  chunk with an invalid cookie gets the same bounded `:need-cookie` reply.\n   434\t- **Reply chunks** are accepted only for a pending query to that observed\n   435\t  address; the state was allocated by this node's own request.\n   436\t- **Inbound `:store`** re-verifies address against bytes and canonicality\n   437\t  before writing, and is refused (`:ok false`) when `publish?` is false or the\n   438\t  inbound storage bound is reached. Refusal is explicit, never silence.\n   439\t- **Bounds**, all composition data (section 9): pending queries, pending\n   440\t  writes, pending gets, partial messages and bytes, globally and per observed\n   441\t  source; work per step.\n   442\t\n   443\t## 9. Limits and defaults\n   444\t\n   445\t| Datum | Default | Notes |\n   446\t|---|---|---|\n   447\t| `:dao.jing.dht/ack-peers` | 2 | Floor 2, ceiling `k`. |\n   448\t| `:dao.jing.dht/ack-ticks` | 5000 | Acknowledgement deadline per pending write. |\n   449\t| `:dao.jing.dht/get-ticks` | 5000 | Deadline per pending get. |\n   450\t| `:dao.jing.dht/query-ticks`, `/tries` | 500, 3 | Per query, today's figures. |\n   451\t| `:dao.jing.dht/max-message-bytes` | 65536 | Encoded message bound, send and reassembly. S3 measures real covered-index nodes at the default branching factor and confirms or raises this default before S4; it is composition data, not a wire constant. |\n   452\t| `:dao.jing.dht/max-partial-messages` | 64 | Global; per-source share is a quarter. |\n   453\t| `:dao.jing.dht/max-pending-writes`, `/max-pending-gets` | 64, 64 | Beyond: `/busy`, or not found with a `/miss`. |\n   454\t| `:dao.jing.dht/cookie-epoch-ticks` | 60000 | |\n   455\t| `:dao.jing.dht/max-inbound-bytes` | composition's | Inbound `:store` bound; S4. |\n   456\t| datagram budget | 1200 | The socket's `:max-bytes`. |\n   457\t\n   458\tEvery stream the DHT is composed over is an evicting ring: requests,\n   459\tanswers, facts, raw traffic. None is a complete-history log.\n   460\t\n   461\t## 10. Today's code, and the slices that replace it\n   462\t\n   463\tWhat the tree holds on 2026-09-30, all of it replaced by this epic with no\n   464\tcompatibility path:\n   465\t\n   466\t| Today | Fate |\n   467\t|---|---|\n   468\t| `dao.jing.dht/IDhtNet`, `lookup`, `create-content-dht` (a waiting transport protocol; put replicates and get fetches synchronously) | Deleted in S2. |\n   469\t| `dao.jing.dht.node` (`create-node`, `create-content-dht-udp`; JVM only, own `DatagramSocket` and receiver thread, one Transit-JSON map per datagram, node id `sha256(host:port)`, claimed `:from` observed unvalidated, replies matched by id alone, oversize dropped silently) | Deleted in S3. |\n   470\t| `dao.jing.dht.kad` | Kept. |\n   471\t| `test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc`, `test/dao/jing/dht/node_test.cljc`, the fake net in `test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc` | Rewritten in S2 and S3. |\n   472\t\n   473\t### Slice plan\n   474\t\n   475\tOrder: **S1 ∥ S2 → S3 → S4 → S5.** S1 and S2 own disjoint files. No slice\n   476\tbefore S5 touches `yin/repl/*`.\n   477\t\n   478\t**S1 — raw datagram stream.** `dao.stream.datagram` (portable), the three\n   479\thost seams, `dao.stream.base64`, `dao.stream.udp`'s `port-step!`.\n   480\tAcceptance:\n   481\t- Exact byte and observed-source round trips over loopback on JVM, Node and\n   482\t  Dart, through the writer handle and the traffic ring.\n   483\t- `bound`, `bind-failed`, `send-failed` and `closed` are observed as events;\n   484\t  no seam takes a function to invoke.\n   485\t- Hostname destination, bad Base64 and oversize are `invalid-value` with no\n   486\t  send; a closed socket is `closed`; inbound oversize is never deposited.\n   487\t- A slow reader observes `gap` on the traffic ring.\n   488\t- `test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc` and every `dao.stream.remote` test pass\n   489\t  unchanged; host seam tests are rewritten.\n   490\t- Value-channel throughput is measured before and after and reported.\n   491\t\n   492\t**S2 — DHT core on in-memory sockets.** `dao.jing.dht` state and step,\n   493\tlookup as state, pending writes and gets, the store handle, solo mode, the\n   494\tJVM facade, the `:jing/unacknowledged` completion in `dao.jing.content.step`.\n   495\tPeers are wired through ring buffers carrying raw datagram values; S2 needs\n   496\tno socket and no S1. It speaks the section 7 wire for single-datagram\n   497\tmessages, with proven and fresh entries but without the cookie MAC (S4) and\n   498\twithout chunks (S3). Acceptance:\n   499\t- Requests and answers are the exact `:jing/*` shapes; the unmodified content\n   500\t  clients drive a DHT.\n   501\t- With a warm table a write is acknowledged in the step that read it, after\n   502\t  exactly `ack-peers` counted sends, and the fact says `/sent`.\n   503\t- `put-bytes-fn` performs no lookup and no peer wait; the publish/readback\n   504\t  round completes against `:local`.\n   505\t- Each `/unacknowledged` reason of 4.3 is produced by a test, including cold\n   506\t  bootstrap reaching `too-few-peers` at the tick deadline and then\n   507\t  succeeding once peers are proven.\n   508\t- Solo mode composes no socket streams and holds no pending work.\n   509\t- A file-backed local verdict survives restart.\n   510\t- Time advances only by appended ticks; no test sleeps.\n   511\t- The JVM facade has one step owner and respects deadlines.\n   512\t- `dao.jing.md`'s backend list and status are updated.\n   513\t\n   514\t**S3 — real sockets and chunks.** Depends on S1 and S2. Chunk records and\n   515\tthe move onto `dao.stream.datagram` sockets. Loopback only. Acceptance:\n   516\t- Two and three peers exchange small queries and multi-datagram segments\n   517\t  under injected loss and reordering.\n   518\t- The largest legal `:find` reply fits one datagram.\n   519\t- Oversize refuses before any send; malformed, duplicate and inconsistent\n   520\t  chunks stay within every bound.\n   521\t- A chunked write counts a peer only when every chunk answered `ok`.\n   522\t- **One transport-neutral chunk split/absorb utility is used by both\n   523\t  `dao.stream.udp` and the DHT**, each keeping its own wire fields and keys.\n   524\t  **Declared fallback:** if the two identities resist clean parameterization,\n   525\t  the DHT ships its own reassembly and `dao.stream.udp` is left untouched.\n   526\t  The slice report says which was taken and why.\n   527\t- Real covered-index nodes are measured against `max-message-bytes`.\n   528\t- JVM↔Node and JVM↔Dart pass the same wire tests.\n   529\t- `dao.jing.cbor.md` (Remote and DHT) is updated to this wire.\n   530\t\n   531\t**S4 — hardening, lookup repair, storage bound.** Depends on S3. The cookie\n   532\tMAC and the `:need-cookie` gate of section 8, and every bound. Acceptance:\n   533\t- A spoofed source elicits at most one reply no larger than its request, and\n   534\t  allocates nothing.\n   535\t- A reply from an unexpected source is ignored; a claimed address never\n   536\t  enters the table.\n   537\t- A live next-nearest peer is tried after dead peers, and dead peers leave\n   538\t  the table.\n   539\t- Inbound storage at its bound refuses explicitly.\n   540\t- `publish?` false neither originates nor serves.\n   541\t- Non-loopback exposure is permitted only after this slice.\n   542\t\n   543\t**S5 — REPL integration.** Depends on S4 and on the durable-store epic (spec,\n   544\tHEAD, lock, recovery). Acceptance:\n   545\t- Default `mem`; `dht:<dir>` is explicit; with no peers it is solo and opens\n   546\t  no socket.\n   547\t- Publishing requires its own flag, separate from peers, and the REPL states\n   548\t  what will be shared before it shares.\n   549\t- The REPL shows per publication whether it is acknowledged (sent to N\n   550\t  peers) or not and why; no round waits on the network.\n   551\t- Two processes with separate locked directories exchange content.\n   552\t- A reader given a manifest address hydrates and queries a remote index.\n   553\t\n   554\tOut of this epic: pinning, garbage collection, availability repair and\n   555\treconciliation sweeps, NAT meeting, a browser transport, latest-root\n   556\tdiscovery, authenticated node ids.\n   557\t\n   558\t## Relationship to the intake-pool observer\n   559\t\n   560\tThe DHT is a content-store backend, not a stream transport. The intake-pool\n   561\tobserver (`dao.jing/observer-state` / `observe-step!`) materializes whatever\n   562\tthe intake streams carry; whether the content store behind it is a DHT handle\n   563\tis invisible to the observer. Conversely, the DHT replicates content, never\n   564\tstreams: there is no ordered, push-based replication of a writer's log through\n   565\tthis layer and no single-writer authority to coordinate — each node simply\n   566\tstores and fetches content-addressed payloads.\n   567\t\n   568\t## Lineage\n   569\t\n   570\tThe pull-based, content-addressed model is Datomic's storage architecture —\n   571\timmutable segments fetched by digest — placed over a Kademlia grid. The\n   572\tverification-by-hash property is the DHT's own trick: because the key is the\n   573\tdigest of the value, distribution can be adversarial without a trusted\n   574\tdirectory. The acknowledgement follows Kafka's producer contract: a write is\n   575\tacknowledged by what was sent, at a level the composition chooses.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
