{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md | sed -n '100,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"   100\t**Requests and answers are the `dao.jing.content` convention**\n   101\t(`src/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc`), unchanged and exact. The DHT is one more\n   102\tserving half of that convention, a sibling of `serve-step` whose answers may\n   103\tarrive on a later step. The clients are the existing ones:\n   104\t`dao.jing.content.step` (portable, stepped), `dao.jing.content.driver` (JVM\n   105\tblocking, host policy), `dao.jing.content.async` (callback facade). No DHT\n   106\tclient vocabulary exists.\n   107\t\n   108\t```clojure\n   109\t;; requests\n   110\t{:jing/request r :jing/get address}\n   111\t{:jing/request r :jing/put address :jing/bytes b64}\n   112\t{:dao.jing.dht/replicate address}            ; section 4.2; no id, the address is the identity\n   113\t\n   114\t;; answers: exact key sets, no additional keys\n   115\t{:jing/request r :jing/found? b :jing/bytes b64-or-nil}\n   116\t{:jing/request r :jing/result :inserted | :present}\n   117\t{:jing/request r :jing/unacknowledged reason}     ; the one addition, section 4.3\n   118\t```\n   119\t\n   120\t- `r` is self-minted and random, as `dao.jing.content` requires. The DHT keeps\n   121\t  no record of completed ids and refuses no duplicate: correlation and\n   122\t  deduplication are the payload's (`dao.stream.md`, Writing).\n   123\t- Answers carry exactly the keys shown, because the stepped client matches\n   124\t  exact key sets. Everything DHT-specific goes on `:facts`.\n   125\t- A malformed request is dropped without answer, as `serve-step` does.\n   126\t- `:jing/unacknowledged` is one new exact answer shape in the `:jing/*`\n   127\t  convention. `dao.jing.content.step` completes it as\n   128\t  `{:id id :error {:code :dao.jing.content/unacknowledged :reason reason}}`.\n   129\t  A purely local content service never produces it.\n   130\t\n   131\t**Facts** are plain data under `:dao.jing.dht/`, one map per fact, keyed by\n   132\taddress, for whoever reads them:\n   133\t\n   134\t| `:dao.jing.dht/fact` | Also carries | Meaning |\n   135\t|---|---|---|\n   136\t| `:dao.jing.dht/sent` | `/address`, `/peers n` | **The acknowledgement.** Every datagram of the write was handed to the socket toward `n` distinct peers, `n >= ack-peers`. |\n   137\t| `:dao.jing.dht/unacknowledged` | `/address`, `/reason`, `/peers m`, `/local` | The write is not acknowledged. `m < ack-peers` is how many peers it was sent to; `/local` is the local verdict or `nil`. |\n   138\t| `:dao.jing.dht/replicated` | `/address`, `/peers n`, `/confirmed c` | Replication after the acknowledgement has finished. `c` peers replied that they stored it. |\n   139\t| `:dao.jing.dht/miss` | `/address`, `/reason` | A get ended not found: `/exhausted`, `/deadline`, `/solo`, `/busy`. |\n   140\t| `:dao.jing.dht/gap` | `/stream` | Section 2. |\n   141\t\n   142\t`:dao.stream/*` operation outcomes are untouched: these are values on\n   143\tstreams, not outcomes of stream operations.\n   144\t\n   145\t## 4. Writes: local insert, then acknowledged when sent to multiple peers\n   146\t\n   147\tThe owner's contract (2026-09-30), verbatim: \"i want to take the kafka route.\n   148\twrite is acknowledged immediately as long as it is sent out on a network. this\n   149\tworks because the data is written to a peer\"; \"sent to a peer but it must send\n   150\tto multiple peers before it can be considered ack\"; local copy: \"Yes, local +\n   151\tnetwork\". This supersedes the earlier local-verdict acknowledgement.\n   152\t\n   153\t### 4.1 What the acknowledgement asserts\n   154\t\n   155\t**A write is acknowledged when the local insert has succeeded and every\n   156\tdatagram of its store message has been handed to the socket toward at least\n   157\t`ack-peers` distinct peers.**\n   158\t\n   159\t- \"Handed to the socket\" is the raw writer's `append!` answering\n   160\t  `:dao.stream/ok` (`dao.stream.datagram.md` section 4), for every datagram of\n   161\t  the message, chunks included. A peer for which any datagram answered\n   162\t  otherwise is not counted, and the next candidate is tried.\n   163\t- A peer counts only if it is a **proven** routing entry (section 6) that is\n   164\t  **fresh**: a matched reply from it arrived within the last\n   165\t  `cookie-epoch-ticks` by this node's own ticks. That reply is also what\n   166\t  carried the cookie the store uses (section 8). A peer that is not fresh is\n   167\t  pinged first.\n   168\t- The acknowledgement says **sent to N peers**. It never says stored by N\n   169\t  peers, received by N peers, or durable anywhere but locally. The word\n   170\t  \"stored\" appears only in `:dao.jing.dht/confirmed`, and that number is\n   171\t  peers' own replies, which are claims.\n   172\t- `ack-peers` is composition data: default 2, floor 2, ceiling `k`. One peer\n   173\t  is never \"multiple\".\n   174\t- A local collision or an invalid address/bytes pair fails before anything\n   175\t  else, as today.\n   176\t\n   177\t### 4.2 Two entry points, one pending write\n   178\t\n   179\t- **The byte-store handle** (section 5.1). `put-bytes-fn` validates, inserts\n   180\t  into `:local`, appends `{:dao.jing.dht/replicate address}` to the request\n   181\t  stream, and returns the local verdict `:inserted` or `:present` at once.\n   182\t  The return value is the byte-store contract's local verdict. It is **not**\n   183\t  the acknowledgement; the acknowledgement is the `/sent` fact.\n   184\t- **A `:jing/put` request.** The step validates (the one ingress check,\n   185\t  `dao.jing/accept-bytes!`), inserts into `:local`, and holds the answer.\n   186\t  `{:jing/request r :jing/result verdict}` is appended when the write is\n   187\t  acknowledged, and in a DHT composition that answer **is** the\n   188\t  acknowledgement.\n   189\t\n   190\tBoth create or join one pending write per address. A pending write records\n   191\tthe address, the request ids waiting on it, the peers sent to, and its\n   192\tdeadline. It never holds the payload; bytes are read from `:local` when sent.\n   193\t\n   194\t### 4.3 How a write proceeds, and what the caller sees when it cannot\n   195\t\n   196\tA pending write never waits on a lookup to acknowledge:\n   197\t\n   198\t1. **Candidates** are the proven routing entries nearest the address's digest\n   199\t   that this node already knows: a table read, no IO.\n   200\t2. **Send** the store message to fresh candidates, nearest first, until `ack-peers` are counted. With a warm table this happens in the\n   201\t   step that read the request: the acknowledgement is immediate.\n   202\t3. **Too few known peers** (a cold bootstrap, a small table): the write starts\n   203\t   an iterative lookup toward the digest and pings candidates; each newly\n   204\t   proven peer is sent to as it appears.\n   205\t4. **Acknowledge** the moment the count reaches `ack-peers`: append the\n   206\t   `/sent` fact and answer every waiting `:jing/put`.\n   207\t5. **Continue after the acknowledgement**, best effort: finish the lookup and\n   208\t   send to the `k` nearest peers not already sent to, so that a reader's\n   209\t   lookup converges on holders. When done or at the deadline, append\n   210\t   `/replicated`. Nothing about the acknowledgement depends on this.\n   211\t\n   212\tIf the count has not reached `ack-peers` when `ack-ticks` have elapsed since\n   213\tthe write began, the write is **not acknowledged**: append the\n   214\t`/unacknowledged` fact and answer every waiting `:jing/put` with\n   215\t`{:jing/request r :jing/unacknowledged reason}`. The pending write is then\n   216\tforgotten. The local copy stays. Trying again is the caller's policy: append\n   217\tthe replicate request again.\n   218\t\n   219\t| Reason | When | Sent anything? |\n   220\t|---|---|---|\n   221\t| `:dao.jing.dht/solo` | No socket is composed. Answered in the step that reads the request. | No |\n   222\t| `:dao.jing.dht/unpublished` | `publish?` is false (section 4.4). Answered at once. | No |\n   223\t| `:dao.jing.dht/oversize` | The encoded store message exceeds `max-message-bytes`. Answered at once. | No |\n   224\t| `:dao.jing.dht/absent` | A replicate request names an address `:local` does not hold. | No |\n   225\t| `:dao.jing.dht/busy` | `max-pending-writes` is reached. Answered at once. | No |\n   226\t| `:dao.jing.dht/too-few-peers` | The deadline passed with fewer than `ack-peers` counted. `/peers m` says how many. | Possibly, to `m` |\n   227\t\n   228\tSo a caller always sees exactly one of `/sent` or `/unacknowledged` per\n   229\tpending write, unless the request itself was lost to a gap, which the `/gap`\n   230\tfact reports.\n   231\t\n   232\t**Oversize fails explicitly and before the local insert.** A payload whose\n   233\tstore message would exceed `max-message-bytes` is rejected by `put-bytes-fn`\n   234\t(it throws, as for any invalid put) and by the `:jing/put` path (the\n   235\t`/oversize` answer, no local insert), in every mode including solo. A segment\n   236\tis never silently local-only.\n   237\t\n   238\t**No REPL round stalls.** `put-bytes-fn` returns after the local insert and\n   239\tone ring append. A round that publishes and reads its manifest back\n   240\t(`yin.repl.index`) completes against `:local`. The acknowledgement arrives on\n   241\t`:facts` when the runner has sent; the REPL reports it when it observes it.\n   242\t\n   243\t### 4.4 Publication (owner decision 1)\n   244\t\n   245\tDefault: never publish. `:dao.jing.dht/publish?` is a separate declaration,\n   246\tdistinct from configuring peers; bootstrap contacts alone declare nothing.\n   247\t\n   248\t- **`publish?` false:** this node originates nothing and serves nothing it\n   249\t  holds. Every write is `/unpublished`. An inbound `:fetch` is answered not\n   250\t  found; an inbound `:store` is answered not ok. The node still answers\n   251\t  `:ping` and `:find`, and still fetches and caches what it asks for.\n   252\t- **`publish?` true:** everything in `:local` is public. The node replicates\n   253\t  its writes, serves `:fetch` from `:local`, and accepts `:store` within its\n   254\t  storage bound. The composition that sets it states what will be shared: the\n   255\t  whole content of that store.\n   256\t\n   257\tThis is a declassification decision, not authorization. ShiBi is where\n   258\tauthorization arrives.\n   259\t\n   260\t### 4.5 Reads\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md | sed -n '261,430p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md | sed -n '261,430p'\"","aggregated_output":"   261\t\n   262\tA `:jing/get` request reads `:local` first and answers a hit in the same\n   263\tstep. On a miss:\n   264\t\n   265\t- Solo: answer not found, with a `/miss` fact of reason `/solo`.\n   266\t- Otherwise start a pending get: an iterative lookup toward the digest, asking\n   267\t  candidates with `:fetch` as they are proven, at most `alpha` outstanding.\n   268\t- A fetched payload is verified before anything else: strict decode, digest\n   269\t  against the requested address, canonical decode (`dao.jing/accept-bytes!`).\n   270\t  A failure is discarded and the next candidate tried.\n   271\t- A verified payload is inserted into `:local`, then answered found.\n   272\t- Candidates exhausted, or `get-ticks` elapsed: answer not found, with a\n   273\t  `/miss` fact.\n   274\t\n   275\t**Not found from a DHT is not authoritative absence.** It says no reachable\n   276\tcandidate produced a verifiable value in time.\n   277\t\n   278\t## 5. The `dao.jing` adapter, the JVM facade, and time\n   279\t\n   280\t### 5.1 The byte-store handle (owner decision 4)\n   281\t\n   282\t`(dao.jing.dht/store-handle {:local l :requests w :max-message-bytes n})` is\n   283\ta plain-data `dao.jing` handle, portable to every host:\n   284\t\n   285\t- `put-bytes-fn`: section 4.2. Local verdict, immediately.\n   286\t- `get-bytes-fn`: **`:local` only.** Bytes or the caller's sentinel. It never\n   287\t  waits, never returns a marker, and never touches the network.\n   288\t- `close-fn`: closes `:local`.\n   289\t\n   290\tIt works with `dao.jing/materialize!`, `dao.jing/get` and `dao.jing/close!`,\n   291\tand is a valid handle for `dao.jing.content/serve-step`.\n   292\t\n   293\t**A remote miss is staged around the handle, not inside it.** The\n   294\tcomposition issues `:jing/get` through `dao.jing.content.step`, drives the\n   295\tDHT step until the completion lands (the verified bytes are then in\n   296\t`:local`), and reads through the handle again. `dao.data.btree.storage`'s\n   297\thydration cache over `dao.jing.content.async` is the existing consumer shape.\n   298\t\n   299\tThis changes today's behavior, where a DHT `get` fetched synchronously on a\n   300\tmiss. The owner ratified the change.\n   301\t\n   302\t### 5.2 The JVM blocking facade\n   303\t\n   304\tHost policy, JVM only, optional. It owns a driver thread that is the DHT\n   305\tstate's one step owner and the tick adapter's cadence, and it wraps\n   306\t`dao.jing.content.driver` so that a `get-bytes-fn` miss issues `:jing/get`\n   307\tand waits to the driver's request deadline. Its `put-bytes-fn` is 5.1's: it\n   308\treturns the local verdict and does not wait for the acknowledgement. No\n   309\tportable code is written against the facade.\n   310\t\n   311\t### 5.3 Time\n   312\t\n   313\tTime reaches the DHT only as data. The step reads a tick stream the\n   314\tcomposition wires and never a host clock, exactly as `dao.lease.md` (Time)\n   315\trequires of lease code, and it reads the same fact:\n   316\t`{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/tick :dao.lease/reading n}`. Readings never\n   317\tdecrease. Units are the composition's; this contract fixes `{:ms 1}`.\n   318\t\n   319\tEvery duration in this document is a tick difference: query deadlines,\n   320\t`ack-ticks`, `get-ticks`, partial-message eviction, cookie epochs. A step\n   321\tthat has observed no tick expires nothing. A tick gap makes expiry late, not\n   322\twrong. Tests drive time by appending ticks.\n   323\t\n   324\t## 6. Peers and routing\n   325\t\n   326\t- **A node id is not derived from an address.** It is 32 bytes of the key\n   327\t  space, composition data. The recommended form is the self-certifying name\n   328\t  of `dao.stream.remote.md` section 5 (a hash of a public key). Until\n   329\t  authentication exists nothing verifies an id, and a peer may claim any id;\n   330\t  that limit is stated, not hidden.\n   331\t- **A routing entry is `{:id id :host h :port p}` where `h` and `p` are the\n   332\t  address the socket observed**, never an address a payload claims. No wire\n   333\t  message carries the sender's own address.\n   334\t- **An entry is recorded only when proven returnable:** a reply arrives from\n   335\t  the expected observed address matching a pending query, or a request\n   336\t  arrives carrying a valid cookie. Nothing is recorded from an unvalidated\n   337\t  datagram.\n   338\t- Peers listed in a `:find` reply are third-party **hints**: candidates to\n   339\t  ping, never entries.\n   340\t- Replies go to the datagram's observed source.\n   341\t- A proven entry whose id reappears from a different proven address is\n   342\t  replaced: a NAT rebinding looks exactly like that.\n   343\t- The table is `dao.jing.dht.kad`: `k = 20`, XOR distance, least-recently-seen\n   344\t  eviction.\n   345\t- **Lookup is explicit state**: target, shortlist, queried set, outstanding\n   346\t  queries (at most `alpha = 3`). A candidate whose query exhausted its tries\n   347\t  is dead for that lookup and is removed from the table, and the shortlist is\n   348\t  refilled with the next-nearest candidate. A lookup ends when `k` peers have\n   349\t  answered or candidates are exhausted.\n   350\t\n   351\tNAT meeting is not in this epic. The DHT may share a hole-punched socket with\n   352\t`dao.stream.udp` (`dao.stream.datagram.md` section 6), and the identity and\n   353\taddress rules above are what keep wire v1 usable once meeting exists.\n   354\t\n   355\t## 7. The wire (v1)\n   356\t\n   357\tOne CBOR value per datagram, encoded with `dao.stream.cbor` (the stream\n   358\tcodec, canonical profile, available on every host). Not `dao.jing.cbor`, and\n   359\tnot Transit. This is a clean break from today's wire; no fleet exists.\n   360\t\n   361\t```clojure\n   362\t;; request\n   363\t{:dao.jing.dht/v 1\n   364\t :op      :ping | :find | :store | :fetch\n   365\t :q       n                  ; query id: self-minted random safe integer\n   366\t :id      <32 bytes>         ; sender's node id\n   367\t :cookie  <bytes>            ; the cookie this peer last gave the sender; absent on first contact\n   368\t :target  <32 bytes>         ; :find\n   369\t :address a                  ; :store, :fetch\n   370\t :bytes   <byte string>}     ; :store: the canonical payload bytes\n   371\t\n   372\t;; reply\n   373\t{:dao.jing.dht/v 1\n   374\t :op      :reply\n   375\t :q       n\n   376\t :id      <32 bytes>\n   377\t :cookie  <bytes>            ; a cookie for the requester's observed address\n   378\t ;; then exactly one of:\n   379\t :need-cookie true\n   380\t :ok      boolean            ; :ping, :store\n   381\t :peers   [[<32 bytes> host port] ...]   ; :find\n   382\t :found   boolean :bytes <byte string>}  ; :fetch\n   383\t\n   384\t;; chunk\n   385\t{:dao.jing.dht/v 1\n   386\t :op :chunk :q n :dir :request | :reply\n   387\t :part i :parts k\n   388\t :cookie <bytes>             ; :dir :request only\n   389\t :bytes  <byte string>}\n   390\t```\n   391\t\n   392\t- `:dao.jing.dht/v` is the discriminator on a shared socket and the version.\n   393\t  A datagram that does not decode to a map carrying it is not the DHT's and is\n   394\t  dropped silently. An unknown version is dropped without reply.\n   395\t- Payload and chunk bytes are CBOR byte strings on the wire. Base64 appears\n   396\t  only in stream-visible values (`:jing/bytes`, raw datagram events).\n   397\t- Ids and targets are raw 32-byte strings on the wire and hex in state.\n   398\t- **Peers per `:find` reply: at most 8**, as `[id host port]` tuples. The\n   399\t  iterative lookup asks again for more. S3 proves by test that the largest\n   400\t  legal reply (8 peers, IPv6 literals) encodes within `max-datagram`.\n   401\t- **A reply is matched by `[observed source, :q]`** against a pending query\n   402\t  sent to that address. `:q` alone matches nothing.\n   403\t- **Chunks.** A message whose encoding exceeds the datagram budget is sent as\n   404\t  `:chunk` records, each fitting the budget. Reassembly is keyed\n   405\t  `[observed host, observed port, :dir, :q]`. Duplicates are ignored;\n   406\t  a `:parts` disagreeing with the held count is dropped; accumulated bytes\n   407\t  over `max-message-bytes` drop the partial; incomplete partials are evicted\n   408\t  oldest-first past `max-partial-messages` and by age in ticks. Loss of a part\n   409\t  is loss of the message, recovered by retrying the whole query.\n   410\t- The total encoded size is checked against `max-message-bytes` **before any\n   411\t  chunk is sent**.\n   412\t- The DHT does not use `dao.stream.udp`'s fragment envelope or its\n   413\t  `:dao.stream.remote/id` identity.\n   414\t- **Reliability is the requester's**: each query has a deadline of\n   415\t  `query-ticks` and `tries` attempts, then the peer is unreachable for that\n   416\t  operation.\n   417\t\n   418\t## 8. Hardening\n   419\t\n   420\tImplemented where the DHT reads raw events, never in the raw layer. Non-loopback\n   421\texposure waits for all of it (slice S4).\n   422\t\n   423\t- **Cookie.** `cookie = MAC(epoch-secret, observed-host | observed-port)`,\n   424\t  truncated to 16 bytes. `epoch-secret = MAC(:dao.jing.dht/secret, epoch)`,\n   425\t  `epoch = floor(now / cookie-epoch-ticks)`. A cookie is valid under the\n   426\t  current or the previous epoch. Verification is stateless. The MAC is\n   427\t  HMAC-SHA-256 from each host's library; nothing is hand-rolled.\n   428\t- **Without a valid cookie a request gets one reply: `:need-cookie` plus a\n   429\t  cookie, no larger than the request datagram.** No work, no lookup, no\n   430\t  storage, no reassembly. The requester retries with the cookie; that retry\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
